insert into public.monthly_ai_catalog(exam_id,version,content) values ('primary-step2-november-2026','2026-11-24-v1', '{"id":"primary-step2-november-2026","version":"2026-11-24-v1","title":"TOEFL Primary Step 2 November 2026","sections":[{"id":"reading","title":"Reading"},{"id":"vocabulary","title":"Vocabulary"},{"id":"language","title":"Language Form and Meaning"},{"id":"writing","title":"Writing"},{"id":"speaking","title":"Speaking"}],"objective":[{"id":"reading","title":"Reading","questions":[{"id":"r1","n":1,"text":"What did Ella bring to school?","options":["Sandwiches","Toy cars","Postcards","Medicine"],"key":2,"explanation":"The first sentence says she brought a collection of postcards.","passage":"A Useful CollectionOn Monday, Ella brought a small collection of postcards to school. Her uncle had sent them from different nations. Each card showed a railway station, a bridge, or a famous building. Ella wanted to make a useful display for the class. She put the cards on a large sheet and wrote a short paragraph about each place. Her friend Max measured the paper and helped her leave space between the cards. At first, one card was missing, and Ella felt upset. Max found it inside an empty container under her desk. They attached it to the display immediately. On Friday, Ella explained the pictures to her classmates. Their teacher said the collection helped everyone learn about the world."},{"id":"r2","n":2,"text":"Who had sent the cards to Ella?","options":["Her uncle","Her neighbour","Max","Her teacher"],"key":0,"explanation":"The passage says her uncle had sent the cards.","passage":"A Useful CollectionOn Monday, Ella brought a small collection of postcards to school. Her uncle had sent them from different nations. Each card showed a railway station, a bridge, or a famous building. Ella wanted to make a useful display for the class. She put the cards on a large sheet and wrote a short paragraph about each place. Her friend Max measured the paper and helped her leave space between the cards. At first, one card was missing, and Ella felt upset. Max found it inside an empty container under her desk. They attached it to the display immediately. On Friday, Ella explained the pictures to her classmates. Their teacher said the collection helped everyone learn about the world."},{"id":"r3","n":3,"text":"Which place was shown on some of the cards?","options":["A railway station","A swimming pool","A picnic shelter","A dentist''s room"],"key":0,"explanation":"The cards showed a railway station, a bridge or a famous building.","passage":"A Useful CollectionOn Monday, Ella brought a small collection of postcards to school. Her uncle had sent them from different nations. Each card showed a railway station, a bridge, or a famous building. Ella wanted to make a useful display for the class. She put the cards on a large sheet and wrote a short paragraph about each place. Her friend Max measured the paper and helped her leave space between the cards. At first, one card was missing, and Ella felt upset. Max found it inside an empty container under her desk. They attached it to the display immediately. On Friday, Ella explained the pictures to her classmates. Their teacher said the collection helped everyone learn about the world."},{"id":"r4","n":4,"text":"Why did Ella put the cards on a large sheet?","options":["To make a display for the class","To send them back","To hide them from Max","To wrap a present"],"key":0,"explanation":"She wanted to make a useful class display.","passage":"A Useful CollectionOn Monday, Ella brought a small collection of postcards to school. Her uncle had sent them from different nations. Each card showed a railway station, a bridge, or a famous building. Ella wanted to make a useful display for the class. She put the cards on a large sheet and wrote a short paragraph about each place. Her friend Max measured the paper and helped her leave space between the cards. At first, one card was missing, and Ella felt upset. Max found it inside an empty container under her desk. They attached it to the display immediately. On Friday, Ella explained the pictures to her classmates. Their teacher said the collection helped everyone learn about the world."},{"id":"r5","n":5,"text":"What did Ella write about each place?","options":["A shopping list","A short paragraph","A medicine label","A train ticket"],"key":1,"explanation":"She wrote a short paragraph about each place.","passage":"A Useful CollectionOn Monday, Ella brought a small collection of postcards to school. Her uncle had sent them from different nations. Each card showed a railway station, a bridge, or a famous building. Ella wanted to make a useful display for the class. She put the cards on a large sheet and wrote a short paragraph about each place. Her friend Max measured the paper and helped her leave space between the cards. At first, one card was missing, and Ella felt upset. Max found it inside an empty container under her desk. They attached it to the display immediately. On Friday, Ella explained the pictures to her classmates. Their teacher said the collection helped everyone learn about the world."},{"id":"r6","n":6,"text":"How did Max help with the paper?","options":["He measured it","He painted it red","He threw it away","He bought a new book"],"key":0,"explanation":"Max measured the paper and helped leave space between the cards.","passage":"A Useful CollectionOn Monday, Ella brought a small collection of postcards to school. Her uncle had sent them from different nations. Each card showed a railway station, a bridge, or a famous building. Ella wanted to make a useful display for the class. She put the cards on a large sheet and wrote a short paragraph about each place. Her friend Max measured the paper and helped her leave space between the cards. At first, one card was missing, and Ella felt upset. Max found it inside an empty container under her desk. They attached it to the display immediately. On Friday, Ella explained the pictures to her classmates. Their teacher said the collection helped everyone learn about the world."},{"id":"r7","n":7,"text":"Why did Ella feel upset at first?","options":["A card was missing","She had missed a train","The teacher was angry","Max had left school"],"key":0,"explanation":"She was upset because one card could not be found.","passage":"A Useful CollectionOn Monday, Ella brought a small collection of postcards to school. Her uncle had sent them from different nations. Each card showed a railway station, a bridge, or a famous building. Ella wanted to make a useful display for the class. She put the cards on a large sheet and wrote a short paragraph about each place. Her friend Max measured the paper and helped her leave space between the cards. At first, one card was missing, and Ella felt upset. Max found it inside an empty container under her desk. They attached it to the display immediately. On Friday, Ella explained the pictures to her classmates. Their teacher said the collection helped everyone learn about the world."},{"id":"r8","n":8,"text":"Where was the missing card found?","options":["In a container under Ella''s desk","At the railway station","Inside Max''s shoe","On the teacher''s chair"],"key":0,"explanation":"Max found it inside an empty container under her desk.","passage":"A Useful CollectionOn Monday, Ella brought a small collection of postcards to school. Her uncle had sent them from different nations. Each card showed a railway station, a bridge, or a famous building. Ella wanted to make a useful display for the class. She put the cards on a large sheet and wrote a short paragraph about each place. Her friend Max measured the paper and helped her leave space between the cards. At first, one card was missing, and Ella felt upset. Max found it inside an empty container under her desk. They attached it to the display immediately. On Friday, Ella explained the pictures to her classmates. Their teacher said the collection helped everyone learn about the world."},{"id":"r9","n":9,"text":"When did Ella explain the pictures to the class?","options":["On Saturday","On Monday morning","On Friday","Every night"],"key":2,"explanation":"The final part says she explained the pictures on Friday.","passage":"A Useful CollectionOn Monday, Ella brought a small collection of postcards to school. Her uncle had sent them from different nations. Each card showed a railway station, a bridge, or a famous building. Ella wanted to make a useful display for the class. She put the cards on a large sheet and wrote a short paragraph about each place. Her friend Max measured the paper and helped her leave space between the cards. At first, one card was missing, and Ella felt upset. Max found it inside an empty container under her desk. They attached it to the display immediately. On Friday, Ella explained the pictures to her classmates. Their teacher said the collection helped everyone learn about the world."},{"id":"r10","n":10,"text":"What is the story mainly about?","options":["A family takes a train journey","A boy loses all his books","Two friends prepare a display that helps their class learn","A teacher purchases a new desk"],"key":2,"explanation":"The postcards, writing and teamwork produce a useful display.","passage":"A Useful CollectionOn Monday, Ella brought a small collection of postcards to school. Her uncle had sent them from different nations. Each card showed a railway station, a bridge, or a famous building. Ella wanted to make a useful display for the class. She put the cards on a large sheet and wrote a short paragraph about each place. Her friend Max measured the paper and helped her leave space between the cards. At first, one card was missing, and Ella felt upset. Max found it inside an empty container under her desk. They attached it to the display immediately. On Friday, Ella explained the pictures to her classmates. Their teacher said the collection helped everyone learn about the world."},{"id":"r11","n":11,"text":"When did Ben help prepare the picnic food?","options":["On Sunday","At midnight","On Friday evening","On Tuesday"],"key":0,"explanation":"The first sentence places the activity on Sunday.","passage":"A Generous PicnicOn Sunday, Ben helped his aunt prepare food for a neighbourhood picnic. They made sandwiches and a large bowl of fruit yoghurt. His aunt asked him to put the yoghurt in a container with a blue lid. Ben picked up a similar container with a red lid instead. It held soup. Before they left, he checked inside and noticed his mistake. He laughed, changed the containers, and put a spoon beside the yoghurt. At the park, a new family had forgotten to bring any food. Ben''s aunt invited them to share their lunch. Everyone sat together, and the children quickly became friends. Ben learned that checking carefully was useful, but being generous made the day even better. He decided to help his aunt again the following weekend."},{"id":"r12","n":12,"text":"Who prepared the food with Ben?","options":["His aunt","His teacher","His dentist","His grandfather"],"key":0,"explanation":"Ben helped his aunt prepare the food.","passage":"A Generous PicnicOn Sunday, Ben helped his aunt prepare food for a neighbourhood picnic. They made sandwiches and a large bowl of fruit yoghurt. His aunt asked him to put the yoghurt in a container with a blue lid. Ben picked up a similar container with a red lid instead. It held soup. Before they left, he checked inside and noticed his mistake. He laughed, changed the containers, and put a spoon beside the yoghurt. At the park, a new family had forgotten to bring any food. Ben''s aunt invited them to share their lunch. Everyone sat together, and the children quickly became friends. Ben learned that checking carefully was useful, but being generous made the day even better. He decided to help his aunt again the following weekend."},{"id":"r13","n":13,"text":"Which two foods did they prepare?","options":["Soup and cake","Apples and noodles","Rice and fish","Sandwiches and fruit yoghurt"],"key":3,"explanation":"They made sandwiches and a bowl of fruit yoghurt.","passage":"A Generous PicnicOn Sunday, Ben helped his aunt prepare food for a neighbourhood picnic. They made sandwiches and a large bowl of fruit yoghurt. His aunt asked him to put the yoghurt in a container with a blue lid. Ben picked up a similar container with a red lid instead. It held soup. Before they left, he checked inside and noticed his mistake. He laughed, changed the containers, and put a spoon beside the yoghurt. At the park, a new family had forgotten to bring any food. Ben''s aunt invited them to share their lunch. Everyone sat together, and the children quickly became friends. Ben learned that checking carefully was useful, but being generous made the day even better. He decided to help his aunt again the following weekend."},{"id":"r14","n":14,"text":"Which lid should be used for the yoghurt container?","options":["No lid","The red lid","A green lid","The blue lid"],"key":3,"explanation":"His aunt specifically asked for the container with a blue lid.","passage":"A Generous PicnicOn Sunday, Ben helped his aunt prepare food for a neighbourhood picnic. They made sandwiches and a large bowl of fruit yoghurt. His aunt asked him to put the yoghurt in a container with a blue lid. Ben picked up a similar container with a red lid instead. It held soup. Before they left, he checked inside and noticed his mistake. He laughed, changed the containers, and put a spoon beside the yoghurt. At the park, a new family had forgotten to bring any food. Ben''s aunt invited them to share their lunch. Everyone sat together, and the children quickly became friends. Ben learned that checking carefully was useful, but being generous made the day even better. He decided to help his aunt again the following weekend."},{"id":"r15","n":15,"text":"What was in the container Ben picked up first?","options":["Sandwiches","Soup","Medicine","Fruit yoghurt"],"key":1,"explanation":"The similar container with a red lid held soup.","passage":"A Generous PicnicOn Sunday, Ben helped his aunt prepare food for a neighbourhood picnic. They made sandwiches and a large bowl of fruit yoghurt. His aunt asked him to put the yoghurt in a container with a blue lid. Ben picked up a similar container with a red lid instead. It held soup. Before they left, he checked inside and noticed his mistake. He laughed, changed the containers, and put a spoon beside the yoghurt. At the park, a new family had forgotten to bring any food. Ben''s aunt invited them to share their lunch. Everyone sat together, and the children quickly became friends. Ben learned that checking carefully was useful, but being generous made the day even better. He decided to help his aunt again the following weekend."},{"id":"r16","n":16,"text":"How did Ben discover his mistake?","options":["A stranger opened his bag","He tasted it at midnight","He checked inside before leaving","He read a note from school"],"key":2,"explanation":"He checked inside before they left and noticed the mistake.","passage":"A Generous PicnicOn Sunday, Ben helped his aunt prepare food for a neighbourhood picnic. They made sandwiches and a large bowl of fruit yoghurt. His aunt asked him to put the yoghurt in a container with a blue lid. Ben picked up a similar container with a red lid instead. It held soup. Before they left, he checked inside and noticed his mistake. He laughed, changed the containers, and put a spoon beside the yoghurt. At the park, a new family had forgotten to bring any food. Ben''s aunt invited them to share their lunch. Everyone sat together, and the children quickly became friends. Ben learned that checking carefully was useful, but being generous made the day even better. He decided to help his aunt again the following weekend."},{"id":"r17","n":17,"text":"What did Ben put beside the yoghurt?","options":["A postcard","A ruler","A spoon","A pencil"],"key":2,"explanation":"He put a spoon beside the yoghurt.","passage":"A Generous PicnicOn Sunday, Ben helped his aunt prepare food for a neighbourhood picnic. They made sandwiches and a large bowl of fruit yoghurt. His aunt asked him to put the yoghurt in a container with a blue lid. Ben picked up a similar container with a red lid instead. It held soup. Before they left, he checked inside and noticed his mistake. He laughed, changed the containers, and put a spoon beside the yoghurt. At the park, a new family had forgotten to bring any food. Ben''s aunt invited them to share their lunch. Everyone sat together, and the children quickly became friends. Ben learned that checking carefully was useful, but being generous made the day even better. He decided to help his aunt again the following weekend."},{"id":"r18","n":18,"text":"Why did the new family need help?","options":["They had missed the train","They had broken a window","They had lost their dog","They had forgotten their food"],"key":3,"explanation":"The new family had forgotten to bring food.","passage":"A Generous PicnicOn Sunday, Ben helped his aunt prepare food for a neighbourhood picnic. They made sandwiches and a large bowl of fruit yoghurt. His aunt asked him to put the yoghurt in a container with a blue lid. Ben picked up a similar container with a red lid instead. It held soup. Before they left, he checked inside and noticed his mistake. He laughed, changed the containers, and put a spoon beside the yoghurt. At the park, a new family had forgotten to bring any food. Ben''s aunt invited them to share their lunch. Everyone sat together, and the children quickly became friends. Ben learned that checking carefully was useful, but being generous made the day even better. He decided to help his aunt again the following weekend."},{"id":"r19","n":19,"text":"What does generous mean in this story?","options":["Afraid of meeting people","Able to run very quickly","Willing to share with other people","Too tired to play"],"key":2,"explanation":"The aunt showed generosity by sharing lunch.","passage":"A Generous PicnicOn Sunday, Ben helped his aunt prepare food for a neighbourhood picnic. They made sandwiches and a large bowl of fruit yoghurt. His aunt asked him to put the yoghurt in a container with a blue lid. Ben picked up a similar container with a red lid instead. It held soup. Before they left, he checked inside and noticed his mistake. He laughed, changed the containers, and put a spoon beside the yoghurt. At the park, a new family had forgotten to bring any food. Ben''s aunt invited them to share their lunch. Everyone sat together, and the children quickly became friends. Ben learned that checking carefully was useful, but being generous made the day even better. He decided to help his aunt again the following weekend."},{"id":"r20","n":20,"text":"What did Ben decide at the end?","options":["To help his aunt again the next weekend","To give away his schoolbag","To stop going to the park","To buy a railway ticket immediately"],"key":0,"explanation":"The final sentence says he decided to help again the following weekend.","passage":"A Generous PicnicOn Sunday, Ben helped his aunt prepare food for a neighbourhood picnic. They made sandwiches and a large bowl of fruit yoghurt. His aunt asked him to put the yoghurt in a container with a blue lid. Ben picked up a similar container with a red lid instead. It held soup. Before they left, he checked inside and noticed his mistake. He laughed, changed the containers, and put a spoon beside the yoghurt. At the park, a new family had forgotten to bring any food. Ben''s aunt invited them to share their lunch. Everyone sat together, and the children quickly became friends. Ben learned that checking carefully was useful, but being generous made the day even better. He decided to help his aunt again the following weekend."}]},{"id":"vocabulary","title":"Vocabulary","questions":[{"id":"v1","n":1,"text":"Our <u>faithful</u> dog waits by the door until we come home.","options":["afraid of people","easy to lose","loyal and dependable","very noisy"],"key":2,"explanation":"A faithful pet stays loyal to its family."},{"id":"v2","n":2,"text":"People from every <u>nation</u> can enter the competition.","options":["a country","a classroom","a sports team","a family name"],"key":0,"explanation":"Here, nation refers to a country."},{"id":"v3","n":3,"text":"Sara keeps her <u>collection</u> of shells in a box.","options":["a kind of weather","a long journey","a place to buy food","a group of things kept together"],"key":3,"explanation":"The shells form a group that Sara keeps."},{"id":"v4","n":4,"text":"The <u>railway</u> connects our town to the city by train.","options":["tracks and a system used by trains","a path inside a house","a road only for bicycles","a place to catch fish"],"key":0,"explanation":"Trains travel on a railway."},{"id":"v5","n":5,"text":"Write a short <u>paragraph</u> about your favourite animal.","options":["a group of sentences about one main idea","a picture without words","a list of numbers","a single letter"],"key":0,"explanation":"A paragraph contains connected sentences."},{"id":"v6","n":6,"text":"Mia felt <u>upset</u> when her favourite toy broke.","options":["unhappy or worried","very hungry","surprised in a happy way","ready to dance"],"key":0,"explanation":"The broken toy made Mia unhappy."},{"id":"v7","n":7,"text":"Please make a <u>photocopy</u> of this worksheet.","options":["a photograph of a mountain","an empty sheet of paper","a paper copy made by a copying machine","a drawing made with paint"],"key":2,"explanation":"A copying machine produces a photocopy."},{"id":"v8","n":8,"text":"The young <u>genius</u> solved the difficult puzzle in one minute.","options":["a person who cannot hear","a person with unusually great ability or intelligence","a person who always arrives late","a person who sells shoes"],"key":1,"explanation":"Solving a very difficult puzzle quickly shows unusual ability."},{"id":"v9","n":9,"text":"Use a ruler to <u>measure</u> the length of the card.","options":["make something much heavier","find the size or amount of something","change the colour of something","hide something carefully"],"key":1,"explanation":"A ruler helps us find length."},{"id":"v10","n":10,"text":"Our <u>international</u> club has members from six countries.","options":["involving more than one country","lasting for one minute","belonging to one family","happening only at night"],"key":0,"explanation":"The club includes people from several countries."},{"id":"v11","n":11,"text":"Leo was <u>generous</u> and shared his lunch with his friend.","options":["always feeling cold","afraid to speak","willing to give or share","unable to remember"],"key":2,"explanation":"Sharing food is a generous action."},{"id":"v12","n":12,"text":"This map is <u>useful</u> because it helps us find the station.","options":["helpful for doing something","difficult to see","too heavy to lift","already broken"],"key":0,"explanation":"The map helps the travellers find their way."},{"id":"v13","n":13,"text":"<u>Whenever</u> it rains, we move our games indoors.","options":["only once before","without knowing why","every time that","a long time after"],"key":2,"explanation":"The games move indoors every time it rains."},{"id":"v14","n":14,"text":"A bus is a <u>vehicle</u> that carries many passengers.","options":["a machine used to carry people or things","a bag used to carry clothes","a building where people sleep","a person who checks tickets"],"key":0,"explanation":"A bus transports people."},{"id":"v15","n":15,"text":"Put the soup in a <u>container</u> with a lid.","options":["a place where trains stop","a person who makes soup","something used to hold or store things","a tool used to cut wood"],"key":2,"explanation":"The container holds the soup."},{"id":"v16","n":16,"text":"The doctor wrote a <u>prescription</u> for the medicine.","options":["a ticket for a concert","a list of classroom rules","a doctor''s written instruction for medicine","a letter inviting a friend"],"key":2,"explanation":"A prescription tells which medicine a patient should receive."},{"id":"v17","n":17,"text":"We will <u>purchase</u> new pencils at the shop.","options":["borrow","throw away","repair","buy"],"key":3,"explanation":"Buying pencils means purchasing them."},{"id":"v18","n":18,"text":"When the alarm rang, everyone left <u>immediately</u>.","options":["right away, without waiting","very quietly","the following week","one person at a time"],"key":0,"explanation":"They left without delay."},{"id":"v19","n":19,"text":"The <u>winner</u> of the race received a gold medal.","options":["the person who finishes first or succeeds in a contest","the person who watches a race","the person who sells the tickets","the person who draws a map"],"key":0,"explanation":"The winner succeeds in the competition."},{"id":"v20","n":20,"text":"The rabbit can <u>hop</u> across the grass.","options":["move with small jumps","walk backwards slowly","sleep for a long time","swim under water"],"key":0,"explanation":"A rabbit moves by making small jumps."},{"id":"v21","n":21,"text":"Mr Lee has a son and a <u>daughter</u>.","options":["a person''s father","a person''s neighbour","a person''s brother","a person''s female child"],"key":3,"explanation":"A daughter is a female child in a family."},{"id":"v22","n":22,"text":"<u>Fasten</u> your seat belt before the car starts.","options":["hold above your head","close or secure firmly","take off and hide","cut into small pieces"],"key":1,"explanation":"Fastening the belt secures it around you."},{"id":"v23","n":23,"text":"<u>Hold on</u> to the rail while the bus is moving.","options":["run as fast as possible","keep a firm grip","let something fall","close both eyes"],"key":1,"explanation":"Holding the rail firmly helps the passenger stay steady."},{"id":"v24","n":24,"text":"My uncle gave me a <u>present</u> for my birthday.","options":["a loud noise","a gift","a difficult job","a short message"],"key":1,"explanation":"Something given for a birthday is a gift."},{"id":"v25","n":25,"text":"The little plant is still <u>alive</u> and has new leaves.","options":["completely dry","too old to use","living, not dead","made of plastic"],"key":2,"explanation":"The new leaves show that the plant is living."},{"id":"v26","n":26,"text":"The <u>dentist</u> checked my teeth yesterday.","options":["a person who looks after teeth","a person who builds houses","a person who takes photographs","a person who drives a bus"],"key":0,"explanation":"A dentist treats and checks teeth."},{"id":"v27","n":27,"text":"<u>Look out</u>! There is a bicycle coming towards us.","options":["look at the stars","be careful of danger","open every window","walk much more slowly"],"key":1,"explanation":"The speaker warns someone about an approaching bicycle."},{"id":"v28","n":28,"text":"The clock showed twelve at <u>midnight</u>.","options":["twelve o''clock at night","twelve o''clock in the daytime","the time when the sun rises","the first hour after school"],"key":0,"explanation":"Midnight is twelve at night; noon is twelve in the daytime."},{"id":"v29","n":29,"text":"Water is a <u>liquid</u>, so it takes the shape of its container.","options":["a kind of soft cloth","a substance that flows and can be poured","a tool for finding directions","something with a fixed hard shape"],"key":1,"explanation":"A liquid flows and takes the shape of its container."},{"id":"v30","n":30,"text":"Please <u>remember</u> to bring your library book tomorrow.","options":["make something new","keep something in your mind or not forget","ask someone to leave","say something very loudly"],"key":1,"explanation":"The speaker asks the student not to forget the book."}]},{"id":"language","title":"Language Form and Meaning","questions":[{"id":"g1","n":1,"text":"Every morning, Ella _____ her lunch in a small container.","options":["is putting","putting","put","puts"],"key":3,"explanation":"Every morning describes a habit. Use puts with a singular subject such as Ella.","tense":"Present Simple"},{"id":"g2","n":2,"text":"Our neighbours usually _____ the train to work.","options":["took","are taking","take","takes"],"key":2,"explanation":"Usually describes a routine; the plural subject takes take.","tense":"Present Simple"},{"id":"g3","n":3,"text":"My brother _____ postcards from different countries.","options":["is collecting","collect","collected","collects"],"key":3,"explanation":"This describes his regular hobby. A singular subject takes collects.","tense":"Present Simple"},{"id":"g4","n":4,"text":"The library _____ at nine o''clock every weekday.","options":["opened","is opening","open","opens"],"key":3,"explanation":"Every weekday shows a regular timetable.","tense":"Present Simple"},{"id":"g5","n":5,"text":"I always _____ my seat belt in the car.","options":["fastens","am fastening","fasten","fastened"],"key":2,"explanation":"Always describes a habit. Use fasten with I.","tense":"Present Simple"},{"id":"g6","n":6,"text":"We _____ our grandparents on most Sundays.","options":["visited","are visiting","visits","visit"],"key":3,"explanation":"Most Sundays describes a repeated action.","tense":"Present Simple"},{"id":"g7","n":7,"text":"Yesterday, Max _____ a ruler at the shop.","options":["bought","buys","is buying","buy"],"key":0,"explanation":"Yesterday requires the past form bought.","tense":"Past Simple"},{"id":"g8","n":8,"text":"Last Friday, our class _____ a new display.","options":["is making","make","makes","made"],"key":3,"explanation":"Last Friday is a finished past time.","tense":"Past Simple"},{"id":"g9","n":9,"text":"Mum _____ the dentist two days ago.","options":["is calling","call","calls","called"],"key":3,"explanation":"Two days ago requires a past-tense verb.","tense":"Past Simple"},{"id":"g10","n":10,"text":"We _____ a kind boy at the bus stop yesterday.","options":["meet","met","are meeting","meets"],"key":1,"explanation":"Met is the past form of meet.","tense":"Past Simple"},{"id":"g11","n":11,"text":"Last night, I _____ my lost pencil under the sofa.","options":["finds","am finding","found","find"],"key":2,"explanation":"Last night requires the past form found.","tense":"Past Simple"},{"id":"g12","n":12,"text":"The children _____ their lunch at noon yesterday.","options":["are eating","ate","eat","eats"],"key":1,"explanation":"Ate is the past form of eat.","tense":"Past Simple"},{"id":"g13","n":13,"text":"Look! The girl _____ across the playground now.","options":["hop","hops","is hopping","hopped"],"key":2,"explanation":"Look and now show an action happening at this moment.","tense":"Present Continuous"},{"id":"g14","n":14,"text":"Right now, the boys _____ the classroom.","options":["measures","are measuring","measure","measured"],"key":1,"explanation":"The plural subject takes are plus measuring for an action happening now.","tense":"Present Continuous"},{"id":"g15","n":15,"text":"Be quiet! My daughter _____ at the moment.","options":["is sleeping","sleep","slept","sleeps"],"key":0,"explanation":"At the moment requires the present continuous.","tense":"Present Continuous"},{"id":"g16","n":16,"text":"At this moment, I _____ a paragraph about my friend.","options":["write","writes","wrote","am writing"],"key":3,"explanation":"With I, use am writing for an action happening now.","tense":"Present Continuous"},{"id":"g17","n":17,"text":"Listen! Our teacher _____ the winner''s name now.","options":["is reading","reads","reading","read"],"key":0,"explanation":"Listen and now show a current action.","tense":"Present Continuous"},{"id":"g18","n":18,"text":"The children _____ yoghurt into bowls right now.","options":["are putting","puts","putting","put"],"key":0,"explanation":"Use are putting with the plural subject and right now.","tense":"Present Continuous"},{"id":"g19","n":19,"text":"At six yesterday evening, Dad _____ soup.","options":["is making","was making","were making","makes"],"key":1,"explanation":"A continuing action at a stated past time takes was making with Dad.","tense":"Past Continuous"},{"id":"g20","n":20,"text":"The children _____ at the stop when the bus arrived.","options":["wait","are waiting","was waiting","were waiting"],"key":3,"explanation":"The waiting was continuing when the bus arrived.","tense":"Past Continuous"},{"id":"g21","n":21,"text":"I _____ a book when the doorbell rang last night.","options":["am reading","read","was reading","were reading"],"key":2,"explanation":"Use was reading for the action in progress when the bell rang.","tense":"Past Continuous"},{"id":"g22","n":22,"text":"At noon yesterday, we _____ our lunch.","options":["was eating","eat","were eating","are eating"],"key":2,"explanation":"A continuing past action with we takes were eating.","tense":"Past Continuous"},{"id":"g23","n":23,"text":"Lily _____ her umbrella when she saw Maya yesterday.","options":["is opening","was opening","were opening","opens"],"key":1,"explanation":"The opening was in progress at that past moment.","tense":"Past Continuous"},{"id":"g24","n":24,"text":"The dogs _____ in the garden when it started to rain.","options":["were playing","was playing","are playing","play"],"key":0,"explanation":"The plural subject and interrupted past activity require were playing.","tense":"Past Continuous"},{"id":"g25","n":25,"text":"I _____ this story twice so far.","options":["have read","am reading","has read","reads"],"key":0,"explanation":"So far links the experience to the present. I takes have read.","tense":"Present Perfect"},{"id":"g26","n":26,"text":"She _____ her ticket, so she cannot travel now.","options":["is losing","have lost","has lost","lose"],"key":2,"explanation":"The lost ticket has a present result. She takes has lost.","tense":"Present Perfect"},{"id":"g27","n":27,"text":"We _____ three new words so far today.","options":["learns","have learned","are learning","has learned"],"key":1,"explanation":"So far today describes completed learning up to now.","tense":"Present Perfect"},{"id":"g28","n":28,"text":"Ben _____ his homework already.","options":["has finished","is finishing","have finished","finish"],"key":0,"explanation":"Already and the singular subject fit has finished.","tense":"Present Perfect"},{"id":"g29","n":29,"text":"They _____ in this town since 2022.","options":["are living","has lived","have lived","lives"],"key":2,"explanation":"Since 2022 connects a past starting point to the present.","tense":"Present Perfect"},{"id":"g30","n":30,"text":"My aunt _____ that country three times.","options":["has visited","have visited","visiting","is visiting"],"key":0,"explanation":"This describes her experience up to now.","tense":"Present Perfect"},{"id":"g31","n":31,"text":"By the time the lesson began, we _____ our books.","options":["are opening","have opened","open","had opened"],"key":3,"explanation":"Opening happened before another past event.","tense":"Past Perfect"},{"id":"g32","n":32,"text":"The bus _____ before Ava reached the stop yesterday.","options":["has left","had left","leaves","is leaving"],"key":1,"explanation":"The bus left before a second event in the past.","tense":"Past Perfect"},{"id":"g33","n":33,"text":"Ben was ready because he _____ his bag the night before.","options":["had packed","has packed","packs","is packing"],"key":0,"explanation":"Packing happened earlier than being ready in the past.","tense":"Past Perfect"},{"id":"g34","n":34,"text":"When I arrived yesterday, Mum _____ the soup already.","options":["had cooked","is cooking","cooks","has cooked"],"key":0,"explanation":"The cooking was complete before the past arrival.","tense":"Past Perfect"},{"id":"g35","n":35,"text":"By the time the picnic started, they _____ all the sandwiches.","options":["had made","are making","have made","make"],"key":0,"explanation":"Making the sandwiches happened before the picnic started.","tense":"Past Perfect"},{"id":"g36","n":36,"text":"She _____ the card before she put it in the envelope yesterday.","options":["has written","had written","is writing","writes"],"key":1,"explanation":"Writing came before putting the card in the envelope in the past.","tense":"Past Perfect"},{"id":"g37","n":37,"text":"Every Saturday, my cousin _____ his faithful dog.","options":["is walking","walked","walks","walk"],"key":2,"explanation":"Every Saturday describes a routine with a singular subject.","tense":"Mixed Review"},{"id":"g38","n":38,"text":"Yesterday afternoon, the train _____ five minutes late.","options":["is arriving","arrives","arrive","arrived"],"key":3,"explanation":"Yesterday afternoon requires a finished past action.","tense":"Mixed Review"}]}],"tasks":[{"id":"writing-story","section":"writing","title":"Picture Story","answerId":"writing-story","rubric":"writing","prompt":"Look at pictures 1-4 in order and write a story of about 6-8 sentences: who the people are, what happens, how they help, and how the story ends. Use linking words. Characters: Lily (red raincoat, yellow umbrella), Sam (blue shirt) and Maya (green shirt), and Maya''s mother. The pictures show: (1) on a cloudy, wet afternoon Lily and Sam walk out of school, Lily carrying a closed yellow umbrella; (2) it is raining hard; under Lily''s umbrella they see Maya sitting alone and worried at a bus stop, holding her books, with no umbrella; (3) Lily, Maya and Sam walk together through the rain under the yellow umbrella, smiling; (4) they arrive at Maya''s house and her mother opens the door, smiling and thanking them. Accept reasonable details consistent with the pictures."},{"id":"writing-essay","section":"writing","title":"My Favourite ...","answerId":"writing-essay","rubric":"writing","prompt":"Choose ONE title: My Favourite Food, My Favourite Place, or My Best Friend. Write an essay with an introduction, three body paragraphs and a conclusion. Give three reasons, explain them and use examples (For example, ...). The page gives a sentence-starter guide (My first reason for choosing ... is that ..., So that is why ..., For this reason, ..., I hope you ...); using those starters is expected and is not copying. Judge whether the student has an introduction, three explained reasons with examples, and a conclusion."},{"id":"speaking-1","section":"speaking","title":"Read Aloud","rubric":"readAloud","prompt":"Read the passage aloud clearly.","target":"A Useful JobLily and Ben are helping in their school garden today. Their teacher has given them a useful job. First, they put young plants into large containers. Ben measures the space between the plants while Lily brings water. A kind neighbour has given the class some seeds, so everyone can grow something different. The children write the names of their plants on small cards. They check that each plant has enough light. Before going home, they collect their tools and clean the table. Lily feels happy because a little work can make the garden a lovely place for everyone to enjoy."},{"id":"speaking-2","section":"speaking","title":"Tell a Picture Story","rubric":"speaking","prompt":"Tell the six-picture story using first, next, then, finally. Characters: Ava (yellow shirt), her Dad (blue shirt) and Noah (green shirt). The pictures show: (1) Dad and Ava buy a present, a white box with a red bow, at a gift shop; (2) they sit on a bench at a bus stop with the present next to Ava; (3) the bus comes and they get on, but the present is left behind on the bench; (4) Noah sees the present on the bench and picks it up; (5) Noah runs to the bus door and gives the present back to Ava; (6) Ava holds the present happily and Dad thanks Noah. Accept reasonable details consistent with the pictures."},{"id":"speaking-3","section":"speaking","title":"Watch and Retell","rubric":"speaking","prompt":"Retell the short animated video: who you saw, what happened, and how it ended. In the video a boy is playing a video game at home. His mother comes home and gives him a present in a box. Inside is a puppy. The boy is happy at first, but then he sees the puppy has only three legs and he puts it down and does not want it. The puppy plays with a red ball, falls over and keeps trying. The boy kicks the ball away, but the puppy brings it back and keeps playing happily, and the boy starts to smile. At the end the boy stands up on crutches: he is also missing a leg. He takes the ball and goes outside to play with the puppy. Accept reasonable retellings of these events."}]}'::jsonb) on conflict(exam_id) do update set version=excluded.version,content=excluded.content;
insert into public.monthly_ai_catalog(exam_id,version,content) values ('itp-november-2026','2026-11-24-v1', '{"id":"itp-november-2026","version":"2026-11-24-v1","title":"TOEFL ITP November 2026","sections":[{"id":"vocabulary","title":"Vocabulary"},{"id":"language","title":"Language Form and Meaning"},{"id":"reading","title":"Reading"},{"id":"writing","title":"Writing"},{"id":"speaking","title":"Speaking"}],"objective":[{"id":"vocabulary","title":"Vocabulary","questions":[{"id":"v1","n":1,"text":"Our <u>perception</u> of the room changed when the lights became brighter.","options":["plan for moving objects","ability to remember names","method of measuring distance","way of noticing and understanding things"],"key":3,"explanation":"Perception concerns how sensory information is noticed and understood."},{"id":"v2","n":2,"text":"The camera can <u>detect</u> movement even when the room is dark.","options":["notice the presence of","prevent the movement of","describe the history of","increase the speed of"],"key":0,"explanation":"The camera notices movement; it does not necessarily stop it."},{"id":"v3","n":3,"text":"A flashing light is a <u>signal</u> that the door is about to close.","options":["machine that makes a sound","sign that gives information","rule that cannot be changed","place where people wait"],"key":1,"explanation":"The flashing light communicates information about the door."},{"id":"v4","n":4,"text":"After the fog cleared, the opposite shore became <u>visible</u>.","options":["safe to walk on","able to be seen","close enough to touch","easy to describe from memory"],"key":1,"explanation":"The fog had hidden the shore from view."},{"id":"v5","n":5,"text":"The new machine has greater <u>capabilities</u>: it can measure both heat and light.","options":["abilities to do things","reasons for failing","costs of repairs","differences in appearance"],"key":0,"explanation":"The example names what the machine is able to do."},{"id":"v6","n":6,"text":"The small telescope has <u>limitations</u>; it cannot show very distant objects clearly.","options":["restrictions on what it can do","instructions for using it","parts that can be replaced","reasons why it was invented"],"key":0,"explanation":"The sentence identifies a limit to the telescope''s ability."},{"id":"v7","n":7,"text":"The microphone''s high <u>sensitivity</u> allows it to pick up very quiet sounds.","options":["ability to store many recordings","strength of its outer case","ability to respond to small changes","speed at which it uses power"],"key":2,"explanation":"A sensitive microphone responds even to weak sound."},{"id":"v8","n":8,"text":"The team made a <u>remarkable</u> recovery and won after being far behind.","options":["completely expected","unusually impressive","difficult to understand","short and unsuccessful"],"key":1,"explanation":"Winning from far behind is impressive and worth noticing."},{"id":"v9","n":9,"text":"Measure the wire <u>precisely</u>, because a small error could affect the result.","options":["quickly","secretly","approximately","exactly"],"key":3,"explanation":"The warning about a small error calls for an exact measurement."},{"id":"v10","n":10,"text":"The young plants are <u>vulnerable</u> to frost, so we cover them at night.","options":["slow to grow","difficult to find","easily harmed","ready to be moved"],"key":2,"explanation":"The cover protects plants that frost could damage."},{"id":"v11","n":11,"text":"The guide chose a safer <u>route</u> through the mountains.","options":["way of travelling from one place to another","reason for starting a journey","group of people travelling together","record of the weather"],"key":0,"explanation":"A route is the path followed on a journey."},{"id":"v12","n":12,"text":"The sailor kept a journal during his long <u>voyages</u> across the ocean.","options":["sea journeys","periods of training","visits to relatives","reports about trade"],"key":0,"explanation":"Crossing the ocean describes journeys by sea."},{"id":"v13","n":13,"text":"The museum displays equipment used during early polar <u>explorations</u>.","options":["journeys to learn about unfamiliar places","competitions between ships","meetings to arrange trade","attempts to repair old equipment"],"key":0,"explanation":"Exploration involves investigating places that are unfamiliar to the explorers."},{"id":"v14","n":14,"text":"The volunteers <u>established</u> a reading club that still meets every week.","options":["joined","moved","criticised","started"],"key":3,"explanation":"They created the club rather than merely becoming members."},{"id":"v15","n":15,"text":"Small coastal <u>settlements</u> grew into busy towns near the harbour.","options":["agreements between rivals","areas used only for farming","places where people live","payments for goods"],"key":2,"explanation":"The places developed into towns, so the residential meaning is intended."},{"id":"v16","n":16,"text":"The builders are <u>extending</u> the path so that it reaches the station.","options":["moving to another place","checking for damage","making longer","closing to the public"],"key":2,"explanation":"The path will reach farther than it does now."},{"id":"v17","n":17,"text":"The harbour became an important <u>commercial</u> centre where merchants bought and sold goods.","options":["connected with trade","controlled by the army","used for religious events","designed for education"],"key":0,"explanation":"Buying and selling goods are commercial activities."},{"id":"v18","n":18,"text":"Local businesses agreed to <u>finance</u> the repairs by paying for new materials.","options":["make a detailed drawing of","ask for permission for","provide money for","take responsibility for the design of"],"key":2,"explanation":"The businesses supply the money needed for repairs."},{"id":"v19","n":19,"text":"Travellers may <u>encounter</u> unexpected weather on a mountain journey.","options":["prepare carefully for","give a warning about","experience or come across","protect themselves from"],"key":2,"explanation":"To encounter a situation is to meet or experience it."},{"id":"v20","n":20,"text":"An <u>erroneous</u> measurement led the team to the wrong conclusion.","options":["incomplete but useful","unusually detailed","difficult to repeat","incorrect"],"key":3,"explanation":"The measurement was wrong, causing an incorrect conclusion."},{"id":"v21","n":21,"text":"Living near the station is an <u>advantage</u> because the journey to work is shorter.","options":["responsibility","expense","surprise","benefit"],"key":3,"explanation":"A shorter journey is a useful benefit."},{"id":"v22","n":22,"text":"The proposal was <u>controversial</u>: some residents strongly supported it, while others strongly opposed it.","options":["easy to put into practice","kept secret from the public","accepted without discussion","causing disagreement"],"key":3,"explanation":"Strongly opposed views show that the proposal causes disagreement."},{"id":"v23","n":23,"text":"Our closest <u>rival</u> in the competition finished only one point behind us.","options":["supporter","competitor","instructor","organiser"],"key":1,"explanation":"A rival competes for the same success."},{"id":"v24","n":24,"text":"The separate clubs were <u>unified</u> into one larger organisation.","options":["moved abroad","joined together","given new leaders","closed temporarily"],"key":1,"explanation":"Separate groups became one organisation."},{"id":"v25","n":25,"text":"Although the first attempt failed, Amir was <u>determined</u> to try again.","options":["not fully prepared","slightly worried","firmly decided","expected by others"],"key":2,"explanation":"He had made a firm decision not to give up."},{"id":"v26","n":26,"text":"The book contains <u>extensive</u> information about the town, covering its history, buildings, and people.","options":["difficult to read","available only to experts","written a long time ago","large in amount or range"],"key":3,"explanation":"The information covers many aspects of the town."},{"id":"v27","n":27,"text":"I <u>recognised</u> the building immediately because I had visited it before.","options":["reached with difficulty","examined for damage","described to someone else","identified as familiar"],"key":3,"explanation":"The earlier visit made the building familiar."},{"id":"v28","n":28,"text":"Without any evidence, the idea remains <u>speculation</u>.","options":["a proven explanation","a carefully measured result","a guess that is not yet supported","an official instruction"],"key":2,"explanation":"The lack of supporting evidence makes the idea uncertain."},{"id":"v29","n":29,"text":"The label was <u>attached</u> to the box with a piece of string.","options":["compared","delivered","fastened","returned"],"key":2,"explanation":"The string holds the label to the box."},{"id":"v30","n":30,"text":"The unexpected discovery <u>fueled</u> public interest in the old ship.","options":["measured","increased","replaced","limited"],"key":1,"explanation":"The discovery made people more interested."},{"id":"v31","n":31,"text":"My teacher gave me useful <u>advice</u> about organising my essay.","options":["permission to miss a lesson","suggestions about what to do","a written account of an event","questions with fixed answers"],"key":1,"explanation":"Advice offers guidance rather than a compulsory order."},{"id":"v32","n":32,"text":"At the border, the traveller showed her <u>passport</u>.","options":["ticket for a local bus","map showing the whole route","official document used for international travel","list of things inside a suitcase"],"key":2,"explanation":"A passport is an official travel and identity document."},{"id":"v33","n":33,"text":"After arriving at the hotel, we began to <u>unpack</u>.","options":["look for a place to stay","put names on our bags","take things out of our bags","check the time of our flight"],"key":2,"explanation":"Unpacking reverses the act of putting belongings into bags."},{"id":"v34","n":34,"text":"He was <u>upset</u> when he learned that his favourite club had closed.","options":["ready to leave","unhappy or worried","surprised in a pleasant way","unable to understand the news"],"key":1,"explanation":"The unwanted closure caused distress."},{"id":"v35","n":35,"text":"The teacher praised the students'' respectful <u>behaviour</u> during the visit.","options":["way of acting","level of knowledge","choice of clothing","ability to remember information"],"key":0,"explanation":"Behaviour means how people act."},{"id":"v36","n":36,"text":"Snow is <u>rare</u> in this warm coastal town.","options":["not common","very dangerous","easy to predict","slow to disappear"],"key":0,"explanation":"The warm location makes snowfall unusual."},{"id":"v37","n":37,"text":"The receptionist spoke in a calm and friendly <u>manner</u>.","options":["language","way","place","position"],"key":1,"explanation":"Here manner describes how the receptionist spoke."},{"id":"v38","n":38,"text":"Living away from home helped her develop greater <u>independence</u>.","options":["interest in travelling to other countries","desire to avoid all responsibilities","knowledge of several languages","ability to manage without relying on others"],"key":3,"explanation":"Managing daily life herself increased her ability to rely on herself."},{"id":"v39","n":39,"text":"Please do not <u>interrupt</u> while someone else is explaining an idea.","options":["stop someone by speaking or acting","disagree silently","ask for written instructions","change your opinion"],"key":0,"explanation":"Interrupting breaks the other person''s turn or activity."},{"id":"v40","n":40,"text":"To prevent the illness from spreading, staff had to <u>isolate</u> the affected animals.","options":["watch from a distance","keep separate from the others","give additional food to","move closer together"],"key":1,"explanation":"Separation reduces contact with unaffected animals."},{"id":"v41","n":41,"text":"The schedule is <u>flexible</u>, so we can change the meeting time if necessary.","options":["difficult to understand","able to be changed","planned far in advance","the same for every group"],"key":1,"explanation":"A flexible schedule can be adjusted."},{"id":"v42","n":42,"text":"The <u>previous</u> owner of the house planted these trees before we moved in.","options":["present","wealthiest","earlier","nearest"],"key":2,"explanation":"The owner before the current one is the previous owner."},{"id":"v43","n":43,"text":"After several unsuccessful searches, they <u>eventually</u> found the missing file.","options":["by chance alone","in the end","at the same moment","without difficulty"],"key":1,"explanation":"The result came after a period of attempts."},{"id":"v44","n":44,"text":"The photographs provide <u>evidence</u> that the bridge was damaged before the storm.","options":["instructions for repairing a structure","a promise that something will happen","a list of possible explanations","information supporting a conclusion"],"key":3,"explanation":"The photographs support the conclusion about earlier damage."},{"id":"v45","n":45,"text":"Each handmade bowl is <u>unique</u>; no two have exactly the same pattern.","options":["different from every other one","difficult to use","made from expensive materials","produced very recently"],"key":0,"explanation":"The sentence explicitly states that every bowl differs."},{"id":"v46","n":46,"text":"The <u>intricate</u> pattern contains many small lines joined in a complicated design.","options":["complex and detailed","brightly coloured","very large","unfinished"],"key":0,"explanation":"Many connected details make a design intricate."},{"id":"v47","n":47,"text":"A thick cover helps <u>insulate</u> the container and keeps the water warm.","options":["reduce the movement of heat into or out of","measure the amount of water in","increase the amount of light entering","remove the pressure inside"],"key":0,"explanation":"Insulation slows heat transfer, helping retain warmth."},{"id":"v48","n":48,"text":"The factory can <u>produce</u> five hundred bicycle helmets a day.","options":["sell to tourists","test for safety","send overseas","make"],"key":3,"explanation":"The quantity per day describes manufacturing output."},{"id":"v49","n":49,"text":"The room was <u>sufficiently</u> large for all twenty students to sit comfortably.","options":["surprisingly","enough for the purpose","equally","slightly"],"key":1,"explanation":"The room met the required size for the group."},{"id":"v50","n":50,"text":"Making a reliable map is a <u>process</u> involving observation, comparison, and revision.","options":["single unexpected event","collection of expensive tools","rule applied without change","series of actions leading to a result"],"key":3,"explanation":"Several connected actions produce the map."}]},{"id":"language","title":"Language Form and Meaning","questions":[{"id":"g1","n":1,"text":"Lena''s present routine is simple: she _____ to college every weekday.","options":["walk","walking","to walk","walks"],"key":3,"explanation":"A third-person singular present-tense subject takes walks."},{"id":"g2","n":2,"text":"Listen! Someone _____ on the laboratory door right now.","options":["knock","has knock","is knocking","to knock"],"key":2,"explanation":"Right now calls for the present continuous, is knocking."},{"id":"g3","n":3,"text":"We _____ the museum yesterday afternoon.","options":["visited","visit","visiting","have visit"],"key":0,"explanation":"Yesterday places the completed visit in the simple past."},{"id":"g4","n":4,"text":"By the time the bus arrived, we _____ our lunch.","options":["had finished","finish","have finish","are finishing"],"key":0,"explanation":"The lunch was completed before another past event."},{"id":"g5","n":5,"text":"We have lived in this neighbourhood _____ 2020.","options":["for","during","since","from"],"key":2,"explanation":"Since introduces the starting point of a period continuing to the present."},{"id":"g6","n":6,"text":"At eight last night, I _____ my notes when the lights went out.","options":["was reading","am reading","reads","have read"],"key":0,"explanation":"The past continuous describes the activity in progress when the lights went out."},{"id":"g7","n":7,"text":"If it rains tomorrow, the class _____ indoors.","options":["would met","meeting","will meet","has meet"],"key":2,"explanation":"A real future condition takes a future result, will meet."},{"id":"g8","n":8,"text":"If I had enough money now, I _____ a better camera.","options":["will bought","buying","had buy","would buy"],"key":3,"explanation":"This imagined present situation uses would plus the base verb."},{"id":"g9","n":9,"text":"If they had left earlier yesterday, they _____ the train.","options":["will catch","would have caught","have catch","would catching"],"key":1,"explanation":"An unreal past result uses would have plus the past participle."},{"id":"g10","n":10,"text":"The bridge _____ by local engineers in 1998.","options":["was designed","designed","was designing","has design"],"key":0,"explanation":"The bridge received the action, so a past passive form is needed."},{"id":"g11","n":11,"text":"These instruments must _____ carefully after the experiment.","options":["clean","be cleaned","cleaning","been clean"],"key":1,"explanation":"A modal passive uses must be plus a past participle."},{"id":"g12","n":12,"text":"Maya is _____ only student who brought a printed map.","options":["a","an","the","no article"],"key":2,"explanation":"The identifies the uniquely specified only student."},{"id":"g13","n":13,"text":"It took us _____ hour to reach the harbour.","options":["a","an","the an","no article"],"key":1,"explanation":"Hour begins with a vowel sound, so an is required."},{"id":"g14","n":14,"text":"We need _____ information before we choose a route.","options":["an","some","many","a few"],"key":1,"explanation":"Information is uncountable, and some can modify an uncountable noun."},{"id":"g15","n":15,"text":"There are _____ paper clips in this box than in that one.","options":["fewer","less","little","much"],"key":0,"explanation":"Paper clips are countable, so fewer is the comparative."},{"id":"g16","n":16,"text":"Neither of the two answers _____ correct.","options":["are being","is","be","have"],"key":1,"explanation":"Neither of two singular alternatives takes is in this sentence."},{"id":"g17","n":17,"text":"The students, along with their teacher, _____ ready to leave.","options":["is","are","be","has"],"key":1,"explanation":"The plural subject is students; the added phrase does not change it."},{"id":"g18","n":18,"text":"This is the scientist _____ discovered the unusual pattern.","options":["who","which","where","whose"],"key":0,"explanation":"Who is the subject of a relative clause referring to a person."},{"id":"g19","n":19,"text":"The library is the place _____ we will meet tomorrow.","options":["where","which in","who","whose"],"key":0,"explanation":"Where introduces a relative clause describing a place."},{"id":"g20","n":20,"text":"The book _____ cover is blue belongs to Sam.","options":["whose","who","which its","where"],"key":0,"explanation":"Whose shows that the cover belongs to the book."},{"id":"g21","n":21,"text":"Although the journey was long, _____ enjoyed it.","options":["but we","we","because we","so that we"],"key":1,"explanation":"Although already introduces the contrast; the main clause begins with we."},{"id":"g22","n":22,"text":"We stayed inside _____ the heavy rain.","options":["because","although","because of","despite of"],"key":2,"explanation":"Because of is followed by the noun phrase the heavy rain."},{"id":"g23","n":23,"text":"_____ she was tired, she completed the report.","options":["Because of","Despite of","During","Although"],"key":3,"explanation":"Although introduces a clause expressing an unexpected contrast."},{"id":"g24","n":24,"text":"The teacher spoke slowly _____ everyone could follow the instructions.","options":["because of","despite","so that","in order"],"key":2,"explanation":"So that introduces a clause expressing purpose."},{"id":"g25","n":25,"text":"You should check the battery _____ you close the switch.","options":["during","before","despite","because of"],"key":1,"explanation":"Before connects the two clauses and establishes their order."},{"id":"g26","n":26,"text":"This route is _____ than the one through the town centre.","options":["safest","more safest","safely","safer"],"key":3,"explanation":"A comparison between two routes uses the comparative adjective safer."},{"id":"g27","n":27,"text":"Of the three maps, this one is _____.","options":["more accurate than","the most accurate","as accurate","accurately"],"key":1,"explanation":"The most accurate identifies the highest degree among three maps."},{"id":"g28","n":28,"text":"The instructions were clear _____ for everyone to understand.","options":["too","enough","such","many"],"key":1,"explanation":"Enough follows the adjective clear."},{"id":"g29","n":29,"text":"The box is _____ heavy for one person to carry safely.","options":["enough","such","many","too"],"key":3,"explanation":"Too heavy expresses an excessive degree."},{"id":"g30","n":30,"text":"She explained the process _____, so we understood every step.","options":["clear","clearness","clearly","clearing"],"key":2,"explanation":"An adverb is needed to describe explained."},{"id":"g31","n":31,"text":"The photograph made the old harbour look _____.","options":["familiarly","familiarity","familiar","familiarise"],"key":2,"explanation":"Look takes an adjective describing the subject''s appearance."},{"id":"g32","n":32,"text":"Our teacher asked us _____ our names on the results sheet.","options":["write","writing","wrote","to write"],"key":3,"explanation":"Ask someone to do something requires the to-infinitive."},{"id":"g33","n":33,"text":"They suggested _____ the experiment with a longer wire.","options":["to repeat","repeat","repeating","repeated"],"key":2,"explanation":"Suggest is followed by an -ing form in this structure."},{"id":"g34","n":34,"text":"We look forward to _____ your presentation next week.","options":["hear","heard","have heard","hearing"],"key":3,"explanation":"To is a preposition in look forward to and is followed by an -ing form."},{"id":"g35","n":35,"text":"The guide let us _____ photographs in the main hall.","options":["take","to take","taking","taken"],"key":0,"explanation":"Let someone do something uses the base verb without to."},{"id":"g36","n":36,"text":"The project involves _____ information from several sources.","options":["collect","to collecting","collected","collecting"],"key":3,"explanation":"Involves is followed by an -ing form describing the activity."},{"id":"g37","n":37,"text":"You _____ use a wall socket for this battery experiment; it is forbidden.","options":["do not have to","might not have","would rather","must not"],"key":3,"explanation":"Must not expresses a prohibition, unlike do not have to."},{"id":"g38","n":38,"text":"I am not certain, but the missing notebook _____ be in the library.","options":["must to","can to","might","should to"],"key":2,"explanation":"Might plus the base verb expresses a possibility."},{"id":"g39","n":39,"text":"You _____ bring tools; the teacher will provide everything needed.","options":["must not to","cannot to","ought not","do not have to"],"key":3,"explanation":"Do not have to expresses that bringing tools is unnecessary."},{"id":"g40","n":40,"text":"Sam has never visited this museum, _____?","options":["hasn''t he","has he","doesn''t he","is he"],"key":1,"explanation":"Never makes the statement negative, so the question tag is positive."},{"id":"g41","n":41,"text":"Let''s compare our results, _____?","options":["will they","do we","are we","shall we"],"key":3,"explanation":"The usual question tag after Let us is shall we."},{"id":"g42","n":42,"text":"She said that she _____ the library the day before.","options":["will visit","had visited","has visit","is visiting"],"key":1,"explanation":"The visit happened before the past reporting verb and is expressed in the past perfect."},{"id":"g43","n":43,"text":"Do you know what time the meeting _____?","options":["does start?","starting","starts","to start"],"key":2,"explanation":"An indirect question uses statement order: the meeting starts."},{"id":"g44","n":44,"text":"The sooner we begin, _____ we will finish.","options":["the sooner","sooner than","soonest","as soon"],"key":0,"explanation":"The repeated comparative pattern is the sooner ... the sooner."},{"id":"g45","n":45,"text":"Not only the map but also the written instructions _____ useful.","options":["were","was","is being","has"],"key":0,"explanation":"The verb agrees with the nearer plural subject, written instructions."},{"id":"g46","n":46,"text":"Everyone should bring _____ own notebook.","options":["their","them","theirs","themselves"],"key":0,"explanation":"Their is the possessive determiner before notebook and can refer to everyone."},{"id":"g47","n":47,"text":"The students completed the whole project by _____.","options":["themselves","their","them","theirs"],"key":0,"explanation":"By themselves means without help from someone else."},{"id":"g48","n":48,"text":"The teacher gave _____ a copy of the instructions.","options":["every students","all student","each student","each of student"],"key":2,"explanation":"Each is followed directly by a singular countable noun."},{"id":"g49","n":49,"text":"I prefer working with a partner _____ working alone.","options":["than","from","as","to"],"key":3,"explanation":"The structure is prefer one activity to another."},{"id":"g50","n":50,"text":"There was _____ noise that we could not hear the announcement.","options":["so many","so much","such many","too many"],"key":1,"explanation":"Noise is uncountable, and the result pattern is so much ... that."}]},{"id":"reading","title":"Reading","questions":[{"id":"r1","n":1,"text":"What is the main purpose of the passage?","options":["To prove that familiar sounds always become quieter","To describe a new treatment for damaged hearing","To explain how the brain selects and interprets everyday sensory information","To argue that all first impressions are accurate"],"key":2,"explanation":"The passage discusses sensory signals, attention, habituation, expectations and communication.","passage":"(1) Imagine moving into a room beside a busy street. On the first evening, every passing bus seems impossible to ignore. A week later, you may read a book without noticing most of the traffic. Yet a quiet knock at the door can still attract your attention. The buses have not necessarily become quieter, and your ears have not simply stopped working. Instead, your brain is treating familiar and unexpected information differently. Experiences like this show that perception involves more than receiving sounds or light. It also involves deciding which signals deserve attention and connecting them with what we already know.(2) The process begins with our sense organs. The eyes respond to light, while the ears respond to changes in air pressure that we experience as sound. Sensory cells help convert this physical information into signals that travel through the nervous system. Different parts of the brain work together to interpret these signals. When you recognise a bicycle, for example, you do not identify it from one feature alone. Its shape, its movement, and your previous experience all contribute. Usually this happens so quickly that you notice the bicycle, not the work involved in recognising it.(3) Because there is more information around us than we can examine carefully, attention has limitations. It helps us select information that matters for a particular activity. A student following a difficult explanation may fail to notice a friend entering the classroom. This does not prove that the friend''s arrival was invisible. The student''s attention was directed towards the explanation. For this reason, repeating the same instruction more loudly is not always the best way to make it clear. First gaining the listener''s attention, and then giving the instruction in manageable parts, may be more useful.(4) Repeated signals can gradually receive less attention when they have no important result. This change is called habituation. It helps explain why the steady sound of a fan may fade into the background while you study. Habituation can be useful because it leaves attention available for other things. However, a new or changing sound may become noticeable again. If the fan suddenly begins to make an unusual noise, you may look up immediately. The change, rather than the usual sound, has become important. Not every ignored signal is harmless, so familiarity should not replace sensible safety checks.(5) Previous experience also creates expectations. Suppose a friend says your name in a crowded room. You may recognise it even when some of the sound is hidden by other voices. Familiarity can help you interpret incomplete information. On another occasion, however, you might think someone has called you when no one has. Expectations can support perception, but they can also lead to an erroneous first impression. Checking a signal through another sense can sometimes help: you might turn around to see whether your friend is looking at you. A first impression is therefore useful information, not a guarantee.(6) Understanding these processes can improve everyday communication. A teacher can reduce unnecessary background sound before explaining a task. A designer can make an important warning clearly different from the signals that appear throughout the day. A student can pause a distracting activity before reading detailed instructions. None of these methods gives us unlimited attention or perfect perception. They simply recognise both the remarkable capabilities and the limitations of the brain. Rather than trying to notice everything at once, we can arrange information so that the details that matter are easier to detect."},{"id":"r2","n":2,"text":"Why does the writer describe buses and a quiet knock in paragraph 1?","options":["To show that an unexpected signal may attract attention when familiar sounds do not","To show that knocks are physically louder than buses","To recommend that students live beside busy roads","To explain how a bus engine produces sound"],"key":0,"explanation":"The contrast introduces the different treatment of familiar and unexpected information.","passage":"(1) Imagine moving into a room beside a busy street. On the first evening, every passing bus seems impossible to ignore. A week later, you may read a book without noticing most of the traffic. Yet a quiet knock at the door can still attract your attention. The buses have not necessarily become quieter, and your ears have not simply stopped working. Instead, your brain is treating familiar and unexpected information differently. Experiences like this show that perception involves more than receiving sounds or light. It also involves deciding which signals deserve attention and connecting them with what we already know.(2) The process begins with our sense organs. The eyes respond to light, while the ears respond to changes in air pressure that we experience as sound. Sensory cells help convert this physical information into signals that travel through the nervous system. Different parts of the brain work together to interpret these signals. When you recognise a bicycle, for example, you do not identify it from one feature alone. Its shape, its movement, and your previous experience all contribute. Usually this happens so quickly that you notice the bicycle, not the work involved in recognising it.(3) Because there is more information around us than we can examine carefully, attention has limitations. It helps us select information that matters for a particular activity. A student following a difficult explanation may fail to notice a friend entering the classroom. This does not prove that the friend''s arrival was invisible. The student''s attention was directed towards the explanation. For this reason, repeating the same instruction more loudly is not always the best way to make it clear. First gaining the listener''s attention, and then giving the instruction in manageable parts, may be more useful.(4) Repeated signals can gradually receive less attention when they have no important result. This change is called habituation. It helps explain why the steady sound of a fan may fade into the background while you study. Habituation can be useful because it leaves attention available for other things. However, a new or changing sound may become noticeable again. If the fan suddenly begins to make an unusual noise, you may look up immediately. The change, rather than the usual sound, has become important. Not every ignored signal is harmless, so familiarity should not replace sensible safety checks.(5) Previous experience also creates expectations. Suppose a friend says your name in a crowded room. You may recognise it even when some of the sound is hidden by other voices. Familiarity can help you interpret incomplete information. On another occasion, however, you might think someone has called you when no one has. Expectations can support perception, but they can also lead to an erroneous first impression. Checking a signal through another sense can sometimes help: you might turn around to see whether your friend is looking at you. A first impression is therefore useful information, not a guarantee.(6) Understanding these processes can improve everyday communication. A teacher can reduce unnecessary background sound before explaining a task. A designer can make an important warning clearly different from the signals that appear throughout the day. A student can pause a distracting activity before reading detailed instructions. None of these methods gives us unlimited attention or perfect perception. They simply recognise both the remarkable capabilities and the limitations of the brain. Rather than trying to notice everything at once, we can arrange information so that the details that matter are easier to detect."},{"id":"r3","n":3,"text":"According to paragraph 2, recognising a bicycle involves:","options":["combining several features with previous experience","identifying its colour and ignoring all other features","waiting until the bicycle stops moving","sending all information to just one brain cell"],"key":0,"explanation":"The paragraph names shape, movement and previous experience as contributing factors.","passage":"(1) Imagine moving into a room beside a busy street. On the first evening, every passing bus seems impossible to ignore. A week later, you may read a book without noticing most of the traffic. Yet a quiet knock at the door can still attract your attention. The buses have not necessarily become quieter, and your ears have not simply stopped working. Instead, your brain is treating familiar and unexpected information differently. Experiences like this show that perception involves more than receiving sounds or light. It also involves deciding which signals deserve attention and connecting them with what we already know.(2) The process begins with our sense organs. The eyes respond to light, while the ears respond to changes in air pressure that we experience as sound. Sensory cells help convert this physical information into signals that travel through the nervous system. Different parts of the brain work together to interpret these signals. When you recognise a bicycle, for example, you do not identify it from one feature alone. Its shape, its movement, and your previous experience all contribute. Usually this happens so quickly that you notice the bicycle, not the work involved in recognising it.(3) Because there is more information around us than we can examine carefully, attention has limitations. It helps us select in