vity before reading detailed instructions. None of these methods gives us unlimited attention or perfect perception. They simply recognise both the remarkable capabilities and the limitations of the brain. Rather than trying to notice everything at once, we can arrange information so that the details that matter are easier to detect."},{"id":"r4","n":4,"text":"In paragraph 3, the word \"limitations\" is closest in meaning to:","options":["instructions","restrictions","advantages","discoveries"],"key":1,"explanation":"Attention cannot examine every piece of information carefully, so it has restrictions.","passage":"(1) Imagine moving into a room beside a busy street. On the first evening, every passing bus seems impossible to ignore. A week later, you may read a book without noticing most of the traffic. Yet a quiet knock at the door can still attract your attention. The buses have not necessarily become quieter, and your ears have not simply stopped working. Instead, your brain is treating familiar and unexpected information differently. Experiences like this show that perception involves more than receiving sounds or light. It also involves deciding which signals deserve attention and connecting them with what we already know.(2) The process begins with our sense organs. The eyes respond to light, while the ears respond to changes in air pressure that we experience as sound. Sensory cells help convert this physical information into signals that travel through the nervous system. Different parts of the brain work together to interpret these signals. When you recognise a bicycle, for example, you do not identify it from one feature alone. Its shape, its movement, and your previous experience all contribute. Usually this happens so quickly that you notice the bicycle, not the work involved in recognising it.(3) Because there is more information around us than we can examine carefully, attention has limitations. It helps us select information that matters for a particular activity. A student following a difficult explanation may fail to notice a friend entering the classroom. This does not prove that the friend''s arrival was invisible. The student''s attention was directed towards the explanation. For this reason, repeating the same instruction more loudly is not always the best way to make it clear. First gaining the listener''s attention, and then giving the instruction in manageable parts, may be more useful.(4) Repeated signals can gradually receive less attention when they have no important result. This change is called habituation. It helps explain why the steady sound of a fan may fade into the background while you study. Habituation can be useful because it leaves attention available for other things. However, a new or changing sound may become noticeable again. If the fan suddenly begins to make an unusual noise, you may look up immediately. The change, rather than the usual sound, has become important. Not every ignored signal is harmless, so familiarity should not replace sensible safety checks.(5) Previous experience also creates expectations. Suppose a friend says your name in a crowded room. You may recognise it even when some of the sound is hidden by other voices. Familiarity can help you interpret incomplete information. On another occasion, however, you might think someone has called you when no one has. Expectations can support perception, but they can also lead to an erroneous first impression. Checking a signal through another sense can sometimes help: you might turn around to see whether your friend is looking at you. A first impression is therefore useful information, not a guarantee.(6) Understanding these processes can improve everyday communication. A teacher can reduce unnecessary background sound before explaining a task. A designer can make an important warning clearly different from the signals that appear throughout the day. A student can pause a distracting activity before reading detailed instructions. None of these methods gives us unlimited attention or perfect perception. They simply recognise both the remarkable capabilities and the limitations of the brain. Rather than trying to notice everything at once, we can arrange information so that the details that matter are easier to detect."},{"id":"r5","n":5,"text":"What can be inferred about the student who misses a friend entering the room?","options":["The student must have poor eyesight","The student may be concentrating successfully on something else","The student does not recognise the friend anymore","The friend must have entered without making any sound"],"key":1,"explanation":"The text explains that attention was directed towards the difficult explanation.","passage":"(1) Imagine moving into a room beside a busy street. On the first evening, every passing bus seems impossible to ignore. A week later, you may read a book without noticing most of the traffic. Yet a quiet knock at the door can still attract your attention. The buses have not necessarily become quieter, and your ears have not simply stopped working. Instead, your brain is treating familiar and unexpected information differently. Experiences like this show that perception involves more than receiving sounds or light. It also involves deciding which signals deserve attention and connecting them with what we already know.(2) The process begins with our sense organs. The eyes respond to light, while the ears respond to changes in air pressure that we experience as sound. Sensory cells help convert this physical information into signals that travel through the nervous system. Different parts of the brain work together to interpret these signals. When you recognise a bicycle, for example, you do not identify it from one feature alone. Its shape, its movement, and your previous experience all contribute. Usually this happens so quickly that you notice the bicycle, not the work involved in recognising it.(3) Because there is more information a