 that you notice the bicycle, not the work involved in recognising it.(3) Because there is more information around us than we can examine carefully, attention has limitations. It helps us select information that matters for a particular activity. A student following a difficult explanation may fail to notice a friend entering the classroom. This does not prove that the friend''s arrival was invisible. The student''s attention was directed towards the explanation. For this reason, repeating the same instruction more loudly is not always the best way to make it clear. First gaining the listener''s attention, and then giving the instruction in manageable parts, may be more useful.(4) Repeated signals can gradually receive less attention when they have no important result. This change is called habituation. It helps explain why the steady sound of a fan may fade into the background while you study. Habituation can be useful because it leaves attention available for other things. However, a new or changing sound may become noticeable again. If the fan suddenly begins to make an unusual noise, you may look up immediately. The change, rather than the usual sound, has become important. Not every ignored signal is harmless, so familiarity should not replace sensible safety checks.(5) Previous experience also creates expectations. Suppose a friend says your name in a crowded room. You may recognise it even when some of the sound is hidden by other voices. Familiarity can help you interpret incomplete information. On another occasion, however, you might think someone has called you when no one has. Expectations can support perception, but they can also lead to an erroneous first impression. Checking a signal through another sense can sometimes help: you might turn around to see whether your friend is looking at you. A first impression is therefore useful information, not a guarantee.(6) Understanding these processes can improve everyday communication. A teacher can reduce unnecessary background sound before explaining a task. A designer can make an important warning clearly different from the signals that appear throughout the day. A student can pause a distracting activity before reading detailed instructions. None of these methods gives us unlimited attention or perfect perception. They simply recognise both the remarkable capabilities and the limitations of the brain. Rather than trying to notice everything at once, we can arrange information so that the details that matter are easier to detect."},{"id":"r7","n":7,"text":"In paragraph 4, \"The change\" refers to:","options":["the student moving to a different room","the fan starting to make an unusual noise","the fan becoming impossible to hear","the student switching off every safety check"],"key":1,"explanation":"The immediately preceding sentence describes the fan beginning to sound unusual.","passage":"(1) Imagine moving into a room beside a busy street. On the first evening, every passing bus seems impossible to ignore. A week later, you may read a book without noticing most of the traffic. Yet a quiet knock at the door can still attract your attention. The buses have not necessarily become quieter, and your ears have not simply stopped working. Instead, your brain is treating familiar and unexpected information differently. Experiences like this show that perception involves more than receiving sounds or light. It also involves deciding which signals deserve attention and connecting them with what we already know.(2) The process begins with our sense organs. The eyes respond to light, while the ears respond to changes in air pressure that we experience as sound. Sensory cells help convert this physical information into signals that travel through the nervous system. Different parts of the brain work together to interpret these signals. When you recognise a bicycle, for example, you do not identify it from one feature alone. Its shape, its movement, and your previous experience all contribute. Usually this happens so quickly that you notice the bicycle, not the work involved in recognising it.(3) Because there is more information around us than we can examine carefully, attention has limitations. It helps us select information that matters for a particular activity. A student following a difficult explanation may fail to notice a friend entering the classroom. This does not prove that the friend''s arrival was invisible. The student''s attention was directed towards the explanation. For this reason, repeating the same instruction more loudly is not always the best way to make it clear. First gaining the listener''s attention, and then giving the instruction in manageable parts, may be more useful.(4) Repeated signals can gradually receive less attention when they have no important result. This change is called habituation. It helps explain why the steady sound of a fan may fade into the background while you study. Habituation can be useful because it leaves attention available for other things. However, a new or changing sound may become noticeable again. If the fan suddenly begins to make an unusual noise, you may look up immediately. The change, rather than the usual sound, has become important. Not every ignored signal is harmless, so familiarity should not replace sensible safety checks.(5) Previous experience also creates expectations. Suppose a friend says your name in a crowded room. You may recognise it even when some of the sound is hidden by other voices. Familiarity can help you interpret incomplete information. On another occasion, however, you might think someone has called you when no one has. Expectations can support perception, but they can also lead to an erroneous first impression. Checking a signal through another sense can sometimes help: you might turn around to see whether your friend is looking at you. A first impression is therefore useful information, not a guarantee.(6) Understanding these processes can improve everyday communication. A teacher can reduce unnecessary background sound before explaining a task. A designer can make an important warning clearly different from the signals that appear throughout the day. A student can pause a distracting activity before reading detailed instructions. None of these methods gives us unlimited attention or perfect perception. They simply recognise both the remarkable capabilities and the limitations of the brain. Rather than trying to notice everything at once, we can arrange information so that the details that matter are easier to detect."},{"id":"r8","n":8,"text":"What does the crowded-room example in paragraph 5 show?","options":["The brain always identifies a familiar voice correctly","It is impossible to recognise names in a crowd","Looking at a friend prevents sound from reaching the ears","Previous experience can help with incomplete information but can also lead to mistakes"],"key":3,"explanation":"The paragraph presents both recognising a name and mistakenly thinking it was called.","passage":"(1) Imagine moving into a room beside a busy street. On the first evening, every passing bus seems impossible to ignore. A week later, you may read a book without noticing most of the traffic. Yet a quiet knock at the door can still attract your attention. The buses have not necessarily become quieter, and your ears have not simply stopped working. Instead, your brain is treating familiar and unexpected information differently. Experiences like this show that perception involves more than receiving sounds or light. It also involves deciding which signals deserve attention and connecting them with what we already know.(2) The process begins with our sense organs. The eyes respond to light, while the ears respond to changes in air pressure that we experience as sound. Sensory cells help convert this physical information into signals that travel through the nervous system. Different parts of the brain work together to interpret these signals. When you recognise a bicycle, for example, you do not identify it from one feature alone. Its shape, its movement, and your previous experience all contribute. Usually this happens so quickly that you notice the bicycle, not the work involved in recognising it.(3) Because there is more information around us than we can examine carefully, attention has limitations. It helps us select information that matters for a particular activity. A student following a difficult explanation may fail to notice a friend entering the classroom. This does not prove that the friend''s arrival was invisible. The student''s attention was directed towards the explanation. For this reason, repeating the same instruction more loudly is not always the best way to make it clear. First gaining the listener''s attention, and then giving the instruction in manageable parts, may be more useful.(4) Repeated signals can gradually receive less attention when they have no important result. This change is called habituation. It helps explain why the steady sound of a fan may fade into the background while you study. Habituation can be useful because it leaves attention available for other things. However, a new or changing sound may become noticeable again. If the fan suddenly begins to make an unusual noise, you may look up immediately. The change, rather than the usual sound, has become important. Not every ignored signal is harmless, so familiarity should not replace sensible safety checks.(5) Previous experience also creates expectations. Suppose a friend says your name in a crowded room. You may recognise it even when some of the sound is hidden by other voices. Familiarity can help you interpret incomplete information. On another occasion, however, you might think someone has called you when no one has. Expectations can support perception, but they can also lead to an erroneous first impression. Checking a signal through another sense can sometimes help: you might turn around to see whether your friend is looking at you. A first impression is therefore useful information, not a guarantee.(6) Understanding these processes can improve everyday communication. A teacher can reduce unnecessary background sound before explaining a task. A designer can make an important warning clearly different from the signals that appear throughout the day. A student can pause a distracting activity before reading detailed instructions. None of these methods gives us unlimited attention or perfect perception. They simply recognise both the remarkable capabilities and the limitations of the brain. Rather than trying to notice everything at once, we can arrange information so that the details that matter are easier to detect."},{"id":"r9","n":9,"text":"Which action best follows the advice in the final paragraph?","options":["Stop a distracting activity before checking important instructions","Make every warning identical to ordinary signals","Try to process every available detail at the same time","Assume familiar signals can never indicate danger"],"key":0,"explanation":"The passage explicitly recommends pausing a distraction to read detailed instructions.","passage":"(1) Imagine moving into a room beside a busy street. On the first evening, every passing bus seems impossible to ignore. A week later, you may read a book without noticing most of the traffic. Yet a quiet knock at the door can still attract your attention. The buses have not necessarily become quieter, and your ears have not simply stopped working. Instead, your brain is treating familiar and unexpected information differently. Experiences like this show that perception involves more than receiving sounds or light. It also involves deciding which signals deserve attention and connecting them with what we already know.(2) The process begins with our sense organs. The eyes respond to light, while the ears respond to changes in air pressure that we experience as sound. Sensory cells help convert this physical information into signals that travel through the nervous system. Different parts of the brain work together to interpret these signals. When you recognise a bicycle, for example, you do not identify it from one feature alone. Its shape, its movement, and your previous experience all contribute. Usually this happens so quickly that you notice the bicycle, not the work involved in recognising it.(3) Because there is more information around us than we can examine carefully, attention has limitations. It helps us select information that matters for a particular activity. A student following a difficult explanation may fail to notice a friend entering the classroom. This does not prove that the friend''s arrival was invisible. The student''s attention was directed towards the explanation. For this reason, repeating the same instruction more loudly is not always the best way to make it clear. First gaining the listener''s attention, and then giving the instruction in manageable parts, may be more useful.(4) Repeated signals can gradually receive less attention when they have no important result. This change is called habituation. It helps explain why the steady sound of a fan may fade into the background while you study. Habituation can be useful because it leaves attention available for other things. However, a new or changing sound may become noticeable again. If the fan suddenly begins to make an unusual noise, you may look up immediately. The change, rather than the usual sound, has become important. Not every ignored signal is harmless, so familiarity should not replace sensible safety checks.(5) Previous experience also creates expectations. Suppose a friend says your name in a crowded room. You may recognise it even when some of the sound is hidden by other voices. Familiarity can help you interpret incomplete information. On another occasion, however, you might think someone has called you when no one has. Expectations can support perception, but they can also lead to an erroneous first impression. Checking a signal through another sense can sometimes help: you might turn around to see whether your friend is looking at you. A first impression is therefore useful information, not a guarantee.(6) Understanding these processes can improve everyday communication. A teacher can reduce unnecessary background sound before explaining a task. A designer can make an important warning clearly different from the signals that appear throughout the day. A student can pause a distracting activity before reading detailed instructions. None of these methods gives us unlimited attention or perfect perception. They simply recognise both the remarkable capabilities and the limitations of the brain. Rather than trying to notice everything at once, we can arrange information so that the details that matter are easier to detect."},{"id":"r10","n":10,"text":"Which statement would the writer most likely agree with?","options":["Louder instructions always solve problems of understanding","Our sense organs provide a complete recording of everything around us","Expectations should never be used when interpreting information","Good communication takes the listener''s available attention into account"],"key":3,"explanation":"The practical examples improve how information is presented rather than claiming unlimited attention.","passage":"(1) Imagine moving into a room beside a busy street. On the first evening, every passing bus seems impossible to ignore. A week later, you may read a book without noticing most of the traffic. Yet a quiet knock at the door can still attract your attention. The buses have not necessarily become quieter, and your ears have not simply stopped working. Instead, your brain is treating familiar and unexpected information differently. Experiences like this show that perception involves more than receiving sounds or light. It also involves deciding which signals deserve attention and connecting them with what we already know.(2) The process begins with our sense organs. The eyes respond to light, while the ears respond to changes in air pressure that we experience as sound. Sensory cells help convert this physical information into signals that travel through the nervous system. Different parts of the brain work together to interpret these signals. When you recognise a bicycle, for example, you do not identify it from one feature alone. Its shape, its movement, and your previous experience all contribute. Usually this happens so quickly that you notice the bicycle, not the work involved in recognising it.(3) Because there is more information around us than we can examine carefully, attention has limitations. It helps us select information that matters for a particular activity. A student following a difficult explanation may fail to notice a friend entering the classroom. This does not prove that the friend''s arrival was invisible. The student''s attention was directed towards the explanation. For this reason, repeating the same instruction more loudly is not always the best way to make it clear. First gaining the listener''s attention, and then giving the instruction in manageable parts, may be more useful.(4) Repeated signals can gradually receive less attention when they have no important result. This change is called habituation. It helps explain why the steady sound of a fan may fade into the background while you study. Habituation can be useful because it leaves attention available for other things. However, a new or changing sound may become noticeable again. If the fan suddenly begins to make an unusual noise, you may look up immediately. The change, rather than the usual sound, has become important. Not every ignored signal is harmless, so familiarity should not replace sensible safety checks.(5) Previous experience also creates expectations. Suppose a friend says your name in a crowded room. You may recognise it even when some of the sound is hidden by other voices. Familiarity can help you interpret incomplete information. On another occasion, however, you might think someone has called you when no one has. Expectations can support perception, but they can also lead to an erroneous first impression. Checking a signal through another sense can sometimes help: you might turn around to see whether your friend is looking at you. A first impression is therefore useful information, not a guarantee.(6) Understanding these processes can improve everyday communication. A teacher can reduce unnecessary background sound before explaining a task. A designer can make an important warning clearly different from the signals that appear throughout the day. A student can pause a distracting activity before reading detailed instructions. None of these methods gives us unlimited attention or perfect perception. They simply recognise both the remarkable capabilities and the limitations of the brain. Rather than trying to notice everything at once, we can arrange information so that the details that matter are easier to detect."},{"id":"r11","n":11,"text":"What is the main focus of the passage?","options":["How travel reports contributed to changing maps and geographical understanding","How to use a modern map during an overseas holiday","Why every early ocean voyage followed the same route","Why Spanish monarchs stopped supporting exploration"],"key":0,"explanation":"All six paragraphs connect voyages with the collection, interpretation and sharing of geographical information.","passage":"(1) A map can look like a final answer about the world, but historical maps often show knowledge in the middle of a change. During the period of Spanish and Portuguese ocean exploration, reports from travellers reached European mapmakers through journals, letters, drawings, and printed accounts. These reports did not always agree. A coastline could be described differently by two sailors, and a familiar name might be used for an unfamiliar place. To create a more reliable map, a mapmaker needed more than an interesting story. Different observations had to be compared, and some earlier ideas had to be revised.(2) The journeys themselves were shaped by practical and political goals. European monarchs supported voyages because new routes might create commercial advantages and increase their power. Sailors also needed to find safe anchorages, fresh water, and a way home. However, money and determination did not remove the limitations of their information. A journey could take longer than expected, supplies could become scarce, and a difficult coastline could be mistaken for a different place. The people planning these voyages therefore had to make decisions before they possessed a complete or accurate picture of the places they hoped to reach.(3) Christopher Columbus''s westward journey in 1492 illustrates the difference between reaching a place and understanding its position in a larger world. His expedition reached islands in the Caribbean, but he interpreted the journey through his expectation of finding a western route to Asia. New observations did not immediately produce a new explanation. The experience also reminds us to be precise about the word \"discovery\". The islands already had Indigenous communities with their own knowledge, names, and histories. A place could be unfamiliar to a European traveller without being unknown to the people who lived there.(4) Later voyages supplied further reports about the Atlantic coasts. Accounts associated with Amerigo Vespucci helped encourage the idea that these lands should be understood as a separate continent rather than part of Asia. In 1507, the German mapmaker Martin Waldseemuller used the name \"America\" on a world map in recognition of Vespucci. The map combined established geographical traditions with information from recent travel. It was not produced by one person simply looking at the whole coastline. It depended on observations travelling from ships to readers, and then being selected and arranged into a new picture.(5) That chain of communication created difficulties as well as opportunities. A sailor might record an uncertain distance, a translator might choose a misleading term, or a reader might accept an exciting claim too quickly. Printed reports could spread an idea widely without proving that every detail was correct. Comparing different sources was therefore important. If two accounts disagreed, the disagreement might reveal an erroneous measurement or a difference in the route followed. A careful reader had to ask what each traveller had actually observed, what had only been inferred, and which details still required further evidence.(6) Seen from this perspective, the history of exploration is also a history of how information is collected and shared. Voyages changed European maps, while maps shaped the questions and plans of later travellers. These exchanges occurred alongside trade, conquest, and serious harm to many Indigenous communities; a more detailed map did not mean that its production or use was fair. Studying the maps critically allows us to recognise both their achievements and their limits. They are valuable not because every line was correct, but because they preserve evidence of people trying to turn partial and sometimes conflicting reports into an understanding of the world."},{"id":"r12","n":12,"text":"According to paragraph 1, why was an interesting travel story not enough to make a reliable map?","options":["Its observations needed to be checked against other information","Mapmakers accepted only accounts written by monarchs","Printed accounts did not exist during that period","Sailors were not allowed to describe coastlines"],"key":0,"explanation":"The paragraph says different reports could disagree and needed comparison.","passage":"(1) A map can look like a final answer about the world, but historical maps often show knowledge in the middle of a change. During the period of Spanish and Portuguese ocean exploration, reports from travellers reached European mapmakers through journals, letters, drawings, and printed accounts. These reports did not always agree. A coastline could be described differently by two sailors, and a familiar name might be used for an unfamiliar place. To create a more reliable map, a mapmaker needed more than an interesting story. Different observations had to be compared, and some earlier ideas had to be revised.(2) The journeys themselves were shaped by practical and political goals. European monarchs supported voyages because new routes might create commercial advantages and increase their power. Sailors also needed to find safe anchorages, fresh water, and a way home. However, money and determination did not remove the limitations of their information. A journey could take longer than expected, supplies could become scarce, and a difficult coastline could be mistaken for a different place. The people planning these voyages therefore had to make decisions before they possessed a complete or accurate picture of the places they hoped to reach.(3) Christopher Columbus''s westward journey in 1492 illustrates the difference between reaching a place and understanding its position in a larger world. His expedition reached islands in the Caribbean, but he interpreted the journey through his expectation of finding a western route to Asia. New observations did not immediately produce a new explanation. The experience also reminds us to be precise about the word \"discovery\". The islands already had Indigenous communities with their own knowledge, names, and histories. A place could be unfamiliar to a European traveller without being unknown to the people who lived there.(4) Later voyages supplied further reports about the Atlantic coasts. Accounts associated with Amerigo Vespucci helped encourage the idea that these lands should be understood as a separate continent rather than part of Asia. In 1507, the German mapmaker Martin Waldseemuller used the name \"America\" on a world map in recognition of Vespucci. The map combined established geographical traditions with information from recent travel. It was not produced by one person simply looking at the whole coastline. It depended on observations travelling from ships to readers, and then being selected and arranged into a new picture.(5) That chain of communication created difficulties as well as opportunities. A sailor might record an uncertain distance, a translator might choose a misleading term, or a reader might accept an exciting claim too quickly. Printed reports could spread an idea widely without proving that every detail was correct. Comparing different sources was therefore important. If two accounts disagreed, the disagreement might reveal an erroneous measurement or a difference in the route followed. A careful reader had to ask what each traveller had actually observed, what had only been inferred, and which details still required further evidence.(6) Seen from this perspective, the history of exploration is also a history of how information is collected and shared. Voyages changed European maps, while maps shaped the questions and plans of later travellers. These exchanges occurred alongside trade, conquest, and serious harm to many Indigenous communities; a more detailed map did not mean that its production or use was fair. Studying the maps critically allows us to recognise both their achievements and their limits. They are valuable not because every line was correct, but because they preserve evidence of people trying to turn partial and sometimes conflicting reports into an understanding of the world."},{"id":"r13","n":13,"text":"In paragraph 2, \"commercial advantages\" means benefits connected with:","options":["religion","language teaching","trade","physical strength"],"key":2,"explanation":"Commercial describes buying, selling and trade.","passage":"(1) A map can look like a final answer about the world, but historical maps often show knowledge in the middle of a change. During the period of Spanish and Portuguese ocean exploration, reports from travellers reached European mapmakers through journals, letters, drawings, and printed accounts. These reports did not always agree. A coastline could be described differently by two sailors, and a familiar name might be used for an unfamiliar place. To create a more reliable map, a mapmaker needed more than an interesting story. Different observations had to be compared, and some earlier ideas had to be revised.(2) The journeys themselves were shaped by practical and political goals. European monarchs supported voyages because new routes might create commercial advantages and increase their power. Sailors also needed to find safe anchorages, fresh water, and a way home. However, money and determination did not remove the limitations of their information. A journey could take longer than expected, supplies could become scarce, and a difficult coastline could be mistaken for a different place. The people planning these voyages therefore had to make decisions before they possessed a complete or accurate picture of the places they hoped to reach.(3) Christopher Columbus''s westward journey in 1492 illustrates the difference between reaching a place and understanding its position in a larger world. His expedition reached islands in the Caribbean, but he interpreted the journey through his expectation of finding a western route to Asia. New observations did not immediately produce a new explanation. The experience also reminds us to be precise about the word \"discovery\". The islands already had Indigenous communities with their own knowledge, names, and histories. A place could be unfamiliar to a European traveller without being unknown to the people who lived there.(4) Later voyages supplied further reports about the Atlantic coasts. Accounts associated with Amerigo Vespucci helped encourage the idea that these lands should be understood as a separate continent rather than part of Asia. In 1507, the German mapmaker Martin Waldseemuller used the name \"America\" on a world map in recognition of Vespucci. The map combined established geographical traditions with information from recent travel. It was not produced by one person simply looking at the whole coastline. It depended on observations travelling from ships to readers, and then being selected and arranged into a new picture.(5) That chain of communication created difficulties as well as opportunities. A sailor might record an uncertain distance, a translator might choose a misleading term, or a reader might accept an exciting claim too quickly. Printed reports could spread an idea widely without proving that every detail was correct. Comparing different sources was therefore important. If two accounts disagreed, the disagreement might reveal an erroneous measurement or a difference in the route followed. A careful reader had to ask what each traveller had actually observed, what had only been inferred, and which details still required further evidence.(6) Seen from this perspective, the history of exploration is also a history of how information is collected and shared. Voyages changed European maps, while maps shaped the questions and plans of later travellers. These exchanges occurred alongside trade, conquest, and serious harm to many Indigenous communities; a more detailed map did not mean that its production or use was fair. Studying the maps critically allows us to recognise both their achievements and their limits. They are valuable not because every line was correct, but because they preserve evidence of people trying to turn partial and sometimes conflicting reports into an understanding of the world."},{"id":"r14","n":14,"text":"Which practical need of sailors is mentioned in paragraph 2?","options":["Taking photographs of every coastline","Building a school at every harbour","Receiving printed weather forecasts","Finding fresh water"],"key":3,"explanation":