ons":["To show that all travellers expected to find an empty continent","To show that reaching a place does not guarantee interpreting it correctly","To prove that no one lived in the Caribbean before 1492","To explain why European rulers no longer wanted new trade routes"],"key":1,"explanation":"His arrival and his expectation of Asia illustrate observation versus interpretation.","passage":"(1) A map can look like a final answer about the world, but historical maps often show knowledge in the middle of a change. During the period of Spanish and Portuguese ocean exploration, reports from travellers reached European mapmakers through journals, letters, drawings, and printed accounts. These reports did not always agree. A coastline could be described differently by two sailors, and a familiar name might be used for an unfamiliar place. To create a more reliable map, a mapmaker needed more than an interesting story. Different observations had to be compared, and some earlier ideas had to be revised.(2) The journeys themselves were shaped by practical and political goals. European monarchs supported voyages because new routes might create commercial advantages and increase their power. Sailors also needed to find safe anchorages, fresh water, and a way home. However, money and determination did not remove the limitations of their information. A journey could take longer than expected, supplies could become scarce, and a difficult coastline could be mistaken for a different place. The people planning these voyages therefore had to make decisions before they possessed a complete or accurate picture of the places they hoped to reach.(3) Christopher Columbus''s westward journey in 1492 illustrates the difference between reaching a place and understanding its position in a larger world. His expedition reached islands in the Caribbean, but he interpreted the journey through his expectation of finding a western route to Asia. New observations did not immediately produce a new explanation. The experience also reminds us to be precise about the word \"discovery\". The islands already had Indigenous communities with their own knowledge, names, and histories. A place could be unfamiliar to a European traveller without being unknown to the people who lived there.(4) Later voyages supplied further reports about the Atlantic coasts. Accounts associated with Amerigo Vespucci helped encourage the idea that these lands should be understood as a separate continent rather than part of Asia. In 1507, the German mapmaker Martin Waldseemuller used the name \"America\" on a world map in recognition of Vespucci. The map combined established geographical traditions with information from recent travel. It was not produced by one person simply looking at the whole coastline. It depended on observations travelling from ships to readers, and then being selected and arranged into a new picture.(5) That chain of communication created difficulties as well as opportunities. A sailor might record an uncertain distance, a translator might choose a misleading term, or a reader might accept an exciting claim too quickly. Printed reports could spread an idea widely without proving that every detail was correct. Comparing different sources was therefore important. If two accounts disagreed, the disagreement might reveal an erroneous measurement or a difference in the route followed. A careful reader had to ask what each traveller had actually observed, what had only been inferred, and which details still required further evidence.(6) Seen from this perspective, the history of exploration is also a history of how information is collected and shared. Voyages changed European maps, while maps shaped the questions and plans of later travellers. These exchanges occurred alongside trade, conquest, and serious harm to many Indigenous communities; a more detailed map did not mean that its production or use was fair. Studying the maps critically allows us to recognise both their achievements and their limits. They are valuable not because every line was correct, but because they preserve evidence of people trying to turn partial and sometimes conflicting reports into an understanding of the world."},{"id":"r16","n":16,"text":"What point does the writer make about the word \"discovery\"?","options":["A place new to Europeans could already be familiar to its inhabitants","The word should refer only to places without water","Indigenous communities used the same names as European mapmakers","A place can have a history only after it appears on a printed map"],"key":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 distinguishes European unfamiliarity from the existing knowledge of Indigenous people.","passage":"(1) A map can look like a final answer about the world, but historical maps often show knowledge in the middle of a change. During the period of Spanish and Portuguese ocean exploration, reports from travellers reached European mapmakers through journals, letters, drawings, and printed accounts. These reports did not always agree. A coastline could be described differently by two sailors, and a familiar name might be used for an unfamiliar place. To create a more reliable map, a mapmaker needed more than an interesting story. Different observations had to be compared, and some earlier ideas had to be revised.(2) The journeys themselves were shaped by practical and political goals. European monarchs supported voyages because new routes might create commercial advantages and increase their power. Sailors also needed to find safe anchorages, fresh water, and a way home. However, money and determination did not remove the limitations of their information. A journey could take longer than expected, supplies could become scarce, and a difficult coastline could be mistaken for a different place. The people planning these voyages therefore had to make decisions before they possessed a complete or accurate picture of the places they hoped to reach.(3) Christopher Columbus''s westward journey in 1492 illustrates the difference between reaching a place and understanding its position in a larger world. His expedition reached islands in the Caribbean, but he interpreted the journey through his expectation of finding a western route to Asia. New observations did not immediately produce a new explanation. The experience also reminds us to be precise about the word \"discovery\". The islands already had Indigenous communities with their own knowledge, names, and histories. A place could be unfamiliar to a European traveller without being unknown to the people who lived there.(4) Later voyages supplied further reports about the Atlantic coasts. Accounts associated with Amerigo Vespucci helped encourage the idea that these lands should be understood as a separate continent rather than part of Asia. In 1507, the German mapmaker Martin Waldseemuller used the name \"America\" on a world map in recognition of Vespucci. The map combined established geographical traditions with information from recent travel. It was not produced by one person simply looking at the whole coastline. It depended on observations travelling from ships to readers, and then being selected and arranged into a new picture.(5) That chain of communication created difficulties as well as opportunities. A sailor might record an uncertain distance, a translator might choose a misleading term, or a reader might accept an exciting claim too quickly. Printed reports could spread an idea widely without proving that every detail was correct. Comparing different sources was therefore important. If two accounts disagreed, the disagreement might reveal an erroneous measurement or a difference in the route followed. A careful reader had to ask what each traveller had actually observed, what had only been inferred, and which details still required further evidence.(6) Seen from this perspective, the history of exploration is also a history of how information is collected and shared. Voyages changed European maps, while maps shaped the questions and plans of later travellers. These exchanges occurred alongside trade, conquest, and serious harm to many Indigenous communities; a more detailed map did not mean that its production or use was fair. Studying the maps critically allows us to recognise both their achievements and their limits. They are valuable not because every line was correct, but because they preserve evidence of people trying to turn partial and sometimes conflicting reports into an understanding of the world."},{"id":"r17","n":17,"text":"According to paragraph 4, why did Waldseemuller use the name \"America\"?","options":["To identify the Spanish monarch who paid for the map","To recognise Vespucci''s contribution to interpreting the new geographical information","To name the ship used on Columbus''s first voyage","To show that the Caribbean was part of Asia"],"key":1,"explanation":"The name recognised Vespucci, whose associated accounts supported the separate-continent interpretation.","passage":"(1) A map can look like a final answer about the world, but historical maps often show knowledge in the middle of a change. During the period of Spanish and Portuguese ocean exploration, reports from travellers reached European mapmakers through journals, letters, drawings, and printed accounts. These reports did not always agree. A coastline could be described differently by two sailors, and a familiar name might be used for an unfamiliar place. To create a more reliable map, a mapmaker needed more than an interesting story. Different observations had to be compared, and some earlier ideas had to be revised.(2) The journeys themselves were shaped by practical and political goals. European monarchs supported voyages because new routes might create commercial advantages and increase their power. Sailors also needed to find safe anchorages, fresh water, and a way home. However, money and determination did not remove the limitations of their information. A journey could take longer than expected, supplies could become scarce, and a difficult coastline could be mistaken for a different place. The people planning these voyages therefore had to make decisions before they possessed a complete or accurate picture of the places they hoped to reach.(3) Christopher Columbus''s westward journey in 1492 illustrates the difference between reaching a place and understanding its position in a larger world. His expedition reached islands in the Caribbean, but he interpreted the journey through his expectation of finding a western route to Asia. New observations did not immediately produce a new explanation. The experience also reminds us to be precise about the word \"discovery\". The islands already had Indigenous communities with their own knowledge, names, and histories. A place could be unfamiliar to a European traveller without being unknown to the people who lived there.(4) Later voyages supplied further reports about the Atlantic coasts. Accounts associated with Amerigo Vespucci helped encourage the idea that these lands should be understood as a separate continent rather than part of Asia. In 1507, the German mapmaker Martin Waldseemuller used the name \"America\" on a world map in recognition of Vespucci. The map combined established geographical traditions with information from recent travel. It was not produced by one person simply looking at the whole coastline. It depended on observations travelling from ships to readers, and then being selected and arranged into a new picture.(5) That chain of communication created difficulties as well as opportunities. A sailor might record an uncertain distance, a translator might choose a misleading term, or a reader might accept an exciting claim too quickly. Printed reports could spread an idea widely without proving that every detail was correct. Comparing different sources was therefore important. If two accounts disagreed, the disagreement might reveal an erroneous measurement or a difference in the route followed. A careful reader had to ask what each traveller had actually observed, what had only been inferred, and which details still required further evidence.(6) Seen from this perspective, the history of exploration is also a history of how information is collected and shared. Voyages changed European maps, while maps shaped the questions and plans of later travellers. These exchanges occurred alongside trade, conquest, and serious harm to many Indigenous communities; a more detailed map did not mean that its production or use was fair. Studying the maps critically allows us to recognise both their achievements and their limits. They are valuable not because every line was correct, but because they preserve evidence of people trying to turn partial and sometimes conflicting reports into an understanding of the world."},{"id":"r18","n":18,"text":"In paragraph 5, \"inferred\" is closest in meaning to:","options":["measured directly with an instrument","copied without changing any words","concluded from available information","deliberately kept secret"],"key":2,"explanation":"An inference goes beyond direct observation by drawing a conclusion from information.","passage":"(1) A map can look like a final answer about the world, but historical maps often show knowledge in the middle of a change. During the period of Spanish and Portuguese ocean exploration, reports from travellers reached European mapmakers through journals, letters, drawings, and printed accounts. These reports did not always agree. A coastline could be described differently by two sailors, and a familiar name might be used for an unfamiliar place. To create a more reliable map, a mapmaker needed more than an interesting story. Different observations had to be compared, and some earlier ideas had to be revised.(2) The journeys themselves were shaped by practical and political goals. European monarchs supported voyages because new routes might create commercial advantages and increase their power. Sailors also needed to find safe anchorages, fresh water, and a way home. However, money and determination did not remove the limitations of their information. A journey could take longer than expected, supplies could become scarce, and a difficult coastline could be mistaken for a different place. The people planning these voyages therefore had to make decisions before they possessed a complete or accurate picture of the places they hoped to reach.(3) Christopher Columbus''s westward journey in 1492 illustrates the difference between reaching a place and understanding its position in a larger world. His expedition reached islands in the Caribbean, but he interpreted the journey through his expectation of finding a western route to Asia. New observations did not immediately produce a new explanation. The experience also reminds us to be precise about the word \"discovery\". The islands already had Indigenous communities with their own knowledge, names, and histories. A place could be unfamiliar to a European traveller without being unknown to the people who lived there.(4) Later voyages supplied further reports about the Atlantic coasts. Accounts associated with Amerigo Vespucci helped encourage the idea that these lands should be understood as a separate continent rather than part of Asia. In 1507, the German mapmaker Martin Waldseemuller used the name \"America\" on a world map in recognition of Vespucci. The map combined established geographical traditions with information from recent travel. It was not produced by one person simply looking at the whole coastline. It depended on observations travelling from ships to readers, and then being selected and arranged into a new picture.(5) That chain of communication created difficulties as well as opportunities. A sailor might record an uncertain distance, a translator might choose a misleading term, or a reader might accept an exciting claim too quickly. Printed reports could spread an idea widely without proving that every detail was correct. Comparing different sources was therefore important. If two accounts disagreed, the disagreement might reveal an erroneous measurement or a difference in the route followed. A careful reader had to ask what each traveller had actually observed, what had only been inferred, and which details still required further evidence.(6) Seen from this perspective, the history of exploration is also a history of how information is collected and shared. Voyages changed European maps, while maps shaped the questions and plans of later travellers. These exchanges occurred alongside trade, conquest, and serious harm to many Indigenous communities; a more detailed map did not mean that its production or use was fair. Studying the maps critically allows us to recognise both their achievements and their limits. They are valuable not because every line was correct, but because they preserve evidence of people trying to turn partial and sometimes conflicting reports into an understanding of the world."},{"id":"r19","n":19,"text":"Why does the writer mention translators in paragraph 5?","options":["To argue that translation always improves the accuracy of a report","To illustrate one way information could change as it passed between people","To show that sailors never wrote their own journals","To explain why mapmakers used only a single language"],"key":1,"explanation":"A misleading translation is one possible problem in the chain of communication.","passage":"(1) A map can look like a final answer about the world, but historical maps often show knowledge in the middle of a change. During the period of Spanish and Portuguese ocean exploration, reports from travellers reached European mapmakers through journals, letters, drawings, and printed accounts. These reports did not always agree. A coastline could be described differently by two sailors, and a familiar name might be used for an unfamiliar place. To create a more reliable map, a mapmaker needed more than an interesting story. Different observations had to be compared, and some earlier ideas had to be revised.(2) The journeys themselves were shaped by practical and political goals. European monarchs supported voyages because new routes might create commercial advantages and increase their power. Sailors also needed to find safe anchorages, fresh water, and a way home. However, money and determination did not remove the limitations of their information. A journey could take longer than expected, supplies could become scarce, and a difficult coastline could be mistaken for a different place. The people planning these voyages therefore had to make decisions before they possessed a complete or accurate picture of the places they hoped to reach.(3) Christopher Columbus''s westward journey in 1492 illustrates the difference between reaching a place and understanding its position in a larger world. His expedition reached islands in the Caribbean, but he interpreted the journey through his expectation of finding a western route to Asia. New observations did not immediately produce a new explanation. The experience also reminds us to be precise about the word \"discovery\". The islands already had Indigenous communities with their own knowledge, names, and histories. A place could be unfamiliar to a European traveller without being unknown to the people who lived there.(4) Later voyages supplied further reports about the Atlantic coasts. Accounts associated with Amerigo Vespucci helped encourage the idea that these lands should be understood as a separate continent rather than part of Asia. In 1507, the German mapmaker Martin Waldseemuller used the name \"America\" on a world map in recognition of Vespucci. The map combined established geographical traditions with information from recent travel. It was not produced by one person simply looking at the whole coastline. It depended on observations travelling from ships to readers, and then being selected and arranged into a new picture.(5) That chain of communication created difficulties as well as opportunities. A sailor might record an uncertain distance, a translator might choose a misleading term, or a reader might accept an exciting claim too quickly. Printed reports could spread an idea widely without proving that every detail was correct. Comparing different sources was therefore important. If two accounts disagreed, the disagreement might reveal an erroneous measurement or a difference in the route followed. A careful reader had to ask what each traveller had actually observed, what had only been inferred, and which details still required further evidence.(6) Seen from this perspective, the history of exploration is also a history of how information is collected and shared. Voyages changed European maps, while maps shaped the questions and plans of later travellers. These exchanges occurred alongside trade, conquest, and serious harm to many Indigenous communities; a more detailed map did not mean that its production or use was fair. Studying the maps critically allows us to recognise both their achievements and their limits. They are valuable not because every line was correct, but because they preserve evidence of people trying to turn partial and sometimes conflicting reports into an understanding of the world."},{"id":"r20","n":20,"text":"Which statement best represents the conclusion?","options":["A detailed map proves that every voyage connected with it was fair","Conflicting reports have no historical value and should be discarded","Every early map provides a fully correct account of the world","Historical maps can be useful evidence even though their contents and uses need critical examination"],"key":3,"explanation":"The conclusion values maps while examining incomplete information and the harmful historical context.","passage":"(1) A map can look like a final answer about the world, but historical maps often show knowledge in the middle of a change. During the period of Spanish and Portuguese ocean exploration, reports from travellers reached European mapmakers through journals, letters, drawings, and printed accounts. These reports did not always agree. A coastline could be described differently by two sailors, and a familiar name might be used for an unfamiliar place. To create a more reliable map, a mapmaker needed more than an interesting story. Different observations had to be compared, and some earlier ideas had to be revised.(2) The journeys themselves were shaped by practical and political goals. European monarchs supported voyages because new routes might create commercial advantages and increase their power. Sailors also needed to find safe anchorages, fresh water, and a way home. However, money and determination did not remove the limitations of their information. A journey could take longer than expected, supplies could become scarce, and a difficult coastline could be mistaken for a different place. The people planning these voyages therefore had to make decisions before they possessed a complete or accurate picture of the places they hoped to reach.(3) Christopher Columbus''s westward journey in 1492 illustrates the difference between reaching a place and understanding its position in a larger world. His expedition reached islands in the Caribbean, but he interpreted the journey through his expectation of finding a western route to Asia. New observations did not immediately produce a new explanation. The experience also reminds us to be precise about the word \"discovery\". The islands already had Indigenous communities with their own knowledge, names, and histories. A place could be unfamiliar to a European traveller without being unknown to the people who lived there.(4) Later voyages supplied further reports about the Atlantic coasts. Accounts associated with Amerigo Vespucci helped encourage the idea that these lands should be understood as a separate continent rather than part of Asia. In 1507, the German mapmaker Martin Waldseemuller used the name \"America\" on a world map in recognition of Vespucci. The map combined established geographical traditions with information from recent travel. It was not produced by one person simply looking at the whole coastline. It depended on observations travelling from ships to readers, and then being selected and arranged into a new picture.(5) That chain of communication created difficulties as well as opportunities. A sailor might record an uncertain distance, a translator might choose a misleading term, or a reader might accept an exciting claim too quickly. Printed reports could spread an idea widely without proving that every detail was correct. Comparing different sources was therefore important. If two accounts disagreed, the disagreement might reveal an erroneous measurement or a difference in the route followed. A careful reader had to ask what each traveller had actually observed, what had only been inferred, and which details still required further evidence.(6) Seen from this perspective, the history of exploration is also a history of how information is collected and shared. Voyages changed European maps, while maps shaped the questions and plans of later travellers. These exchanges occurred alongside trade, conquest, and serious harm to many Indigenous communities; a more detailed map did not mean that its production or use was fair. Studying the maps critically allows us to recognise both their achievements and their limits. They are valuable not because every line was correct, but because they preserve evidence of people trying to turn partial and sometimes conflicting reports into an understanding of the world."}]},{"id":"writing","title":"Writing - Read & Choose","questions":[{"id":"w1","n":1,"text":"Last month, our class _____ a project about changes in the neighbourhood.","options":["starts","started","starting","has start"],"key":1,"explanation":"Last month calls for the simple past, started."},{"id":"w2","n":2,"text":"Our teacher asked us _____ photographs and written records.","options":["to collect","collect","collecting","collected"],"key":0,"explanation":"Asked us to collect is the correct infinitive structure."},{"id":"w3","n":3,"text":"Each group chose _____ place to investigate.","options":["a","an","many","no article"],"key":0,"explanation":"A singular countable noun with an unspecified reference needs a."},{"id":"w4","n":4,"text":"We found two books _____ contained useful information.","options":["who","which","where","whose"],"key":1,"explanation":"Which is the subject of the relative clause and refers to books."},{"id":"w5","n":5,"text":"We took notes carefully _____ we wanted to use our own words.","options":["because of","despite","although of","because"],"key":3,"explanation":"Because introduces a clause giving the reason."},{"id":"w6","n":6,"text":"We asked an older resident to share _____ memories.","options":["their","them","theirs","themselves"],"key":0,"explanation":"Their is the possessive determiner before memories."},{"id":"w7","n":7,"text":"The old photographs were much _____ than we expected.","options":["small","smallest","smaller","more small than"],"key":2,"explanation":"The comparative smaller fits before than."},{"id":"w8","n":8,"text":"We noticed that several shops _____ into flats.","options":["had turning","had been turned","has turned","were turn"],"key":1,"explanation":"The shops received an earlier completed action, expressed by a past-perfect passive."},{"id":"w9","n":9,"text":"We presented our findings _____ Friday afternoon.","options":["in","at","by of","on"],"key":3,"explanation":"On is used with a particular day and part of that day."},{"id":"w10","n":10,"text":"We checked evidence rather than _____ the first explanation.","options":["accepted","to accepting","accepting","accepts"],"key":2,"explanation":"The -ing form accepting matches checking after rather than."}]}],"tasks":[{"id":"writing-email","section":"writing","title":"Email Reply","answerId":"email","prompt":"Reply to Nina. Answer all three questions and give reasons for your suggestions. Use complete sentences and a suitable greeting and closing. You have 7 minutes.From: Nina\nTo: You\nSubject: Help with our welcome afternoon\n\nHi!\nOur language club is planning a welcome afternoon for new students next month. I am helping to organise it, and I would like your ideas. We want students who do not know each other to feel comfortable.\n\nWhat activity should we organise, and why would it help people get to know one another?\nWhat should we ask students to bring?\nIf the outdoor space is unavailable, where else could we hold the event, and why?\n\nPlease write back with your suggestions.\nThanks,\nNinaYour Reply0 words","rubric":"writing"},{"id":"writing-opinion","section":"writing","title":"Opinion Paragraph","answerId":"opinion","prompt":"Some schools set aside a phone-free study period every day so that students can concentrate and talk to one another. Other schools allow students to use their phones during study periods for research and learning activities. Which approach do you think is better, and why?Write about 100-150 words. State your opinion, support it with reasons and a specific example, and finish with a conclusion. Use linking words. You have 10 minutes.Your Paragraph0 words","rubric":"writing"},{"id":"speaking-1","section":"speaking","title":"Read Aloud","prompt":"Read the passage \"How Our Brain Makes Sense of Things\" aloud.","target":"Your eyes and ears help you notice the world, but your brain helps you understand it. When you see a red bicycle, your eyes detect light and send signals to your brain. Your brain connects the shape and colour with things you have seen before. It helps you recognise the bicycle. Attention is important too. If you are reading an interesting book, you may not notice a quiet sound nearby. The sound is still there, but your attention is somewhere else. Sometimes our expectations can also affect what we notice. You might think you hear the school bell because you are waiting for lunch. Looking again or listening carefully can help you check your first idea. Our brains are remarkable, but they do not record every detail. Taking a short break and focusing on one thing at a time can help us notice more.","rubric":"readAloud"},{"id":"speaking-2","section":"speaking","title":"Picture Story","rubric":"speaking","prompt":"Tell the six-picture story, joining the events with linking words. Characters: Aiden (blue hoodie), Mia (yellow T-shirt) and Leo (green T-shirt). The pictures show: (1) at a food stall at an outdoor park festival, Aiden pays with a card while Mia and Leo stand beside him; (2) Mia and Leo sit at a table under a red umbrella and keep a seat for Aiden, who is still at the stall; (3) the stall worker gives Aiden a tray with three wraps and three drinks; (4) Aiden walks to a table under a blue umbrella, but a man and a woman he does not know are sitting there and he looks confused; (5) he notices Mia waving to him from the table under the red umbrella; (6) the three friends eat and laugh together under the red umbrella. Credit the problem (going to the wrong table) and how it is solved. Accept reasonable details consistent with the pictures."},{"id":"speaking-3","section":"speaking","title":"Non-academic Listen-Speak","rubric":"speaking","prompt":"The student heard a teacher''s announcement once and must explain it to an absent classmate, Sam: when and where to meet, including the plan for rain; what students must bring and what the teacher will provide; the group activity; and an important safety instruction. Check every fact against this announcement; credit accurate facts and point out wrong or missing ones. ANNOUNCEMENT: Good afternoon, everyone. On Thursday, November 26th, we will do an outdoor experiment with electromagnets. Meet at the covered tables beside the school garden at 3:30, not in the usual laboratory. Each student should bring a notebook, a pencil, and five steel paper clips in a small plastic box. Wear closed shoes. Please do not bring your own batteries or tools. I will provide battery holders, insulated wire, switches, and iron bolts. We will work in groups of four and compare how many paper clips a coil can lift with different numbers of turns. Take turns connecting the equipment, counting the clips, and recording the results. Ask me to check the circuit before you close the switch. Open the switch between trials and tell me immediately if a wire becomes warm. Never use a wall socket. If it rains, meet at the same time in Room 12 instead. Bring your group''s result sheet to Friday''s class."}]}'::jsonb) on conflict(exam_id) do update set version=excluded.version,content=excluded.content;
insert into public.monthly_ai_catalog(exam_id,version,content) values ('ibt-november-2026','2026-11-24-v1', '{"id":"ibt-november-2026","version":"2026-11-24-v1","title":"TOEFL iBT November 2026","sections":[{"id":"vocabulary","title":"Vocabulary"},{"id":"reading","title":"Reading"},{"id":"writing","title":"Writing"},{"id":"speaking","title":"Speaking"}],"objective":[{"id":"vocabulary","title":"Vocabulary","questions":[{"n":1,"text":"In Passage 1, <u>abandon</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["give up","look into","repair","set up"],"key":0,"explanation":"The college chose not to give up its plan for the courtyard.","id":"v1"},{"n":2,"text":"In Passage 1, <u>acquire</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["forget","describe","display","gain"],"key":3,"explanation":"Students would gain skills through practical gardening.","id":"v2"},{"n":3,"text":"In Passage 1, <u>accurate</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["correct and exact","recently discovered","easy to carry","quickly drawn"],"key":0,"explanation":"Checking each measurement makes the map exact.","id":"v3"},{"n":4,"text":"In Passage 1, <u>adjacent</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["different from","far beyond","next to","hidden beneath"],"key":2,"explanation":"The library is in the building next to the courtyard.","id":"v4"},{"n":5,"text":"In Passage 1, <u>allocate</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["set aside for a purpose","calculate incorrectly","remove completely","collect from visitors"],"key":0,"explanation":"Staff assigned part of an existing budget to tools.","id":"v5"},{"n":6,"text":"In Passage 1, <u>anticipate</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["cause immediately","expect in advance","measure precisely","ignore entirely"],"key":1,"explanation":"Students expected a future water shortage and planned for it.","id":"v6"},{"n":7,"text":"In Passage 1, <u>detect</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["prevent every occurrence of","increase the size of","discover the presence of","make a record of"],"key":2,"explanation":"Inspections helped students find leaks that were already present.","id":"v7"},{"n":8,"text":"In Passage 1, <u>distinct</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["closely connected","clearly different","temporarily closed","equally expensive"],"key":1,"explanation":"The courtyard areas have different, clearly separated purposes.","id":"v8"},{"n":9,"text":"In Passage 1, <u>document</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["delay","record","advertise","question"],"key":1,"explanation":"Photographs and written notes provide a record of changes.","id":"v9"},{"n":10,"text":"In Passage 1, <u>establish</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["take part in","pay a fee to","move away from","set up"],"key":3,"explanation":"They aimed to start a club that would continue into the future.","id":"v10"},{"n":11,"text":"In Passage 2, <u>empirical</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["designed for profit","based on tradition","based on observation","impossible to repeat"],"key":2,"explanation":"The team collected observations instead of relying on opinions.","id":"v11"},{"n":12,"text":"In Passage 2, <u>environment</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["personal ability","final decision","required equipment","surrounding conditions"],"key":3,"explanation":"The study environment includes the conditions in each room.","id":"v12"},{"n":13,"text":"In Passage 2, <u>evaluate</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["decorate","assess","combine","reserve"],"key":1,"explanation":"The team used a task to assess each study room.","id":"v13"},{"n":14,"text":"In Passage 2, <u>exceed</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["depend on","fall below","remain near","go above"],"key":3,"explanation":"The noise could not go above the stated limit.","id":"v14"},{"n":15,"text":"In Passage 2, <u>external</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["heard only by researchers","made by machines","coming from outside","continuing without a break"],"key":2,"explanation":"Road noise came from outside the study rooms.","id":"v15"},{"n":16,"text":"In Passage 2, <u>facilitate</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["make easier","carry out alone","explain afterward","make compulsory"],"key":0,"explanation":"Clear instructions would make the task easier to understand.","id":"v16"},{"n":17,"text":"In Passage 2, <u>fluctuate</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["remain unchanged","rise and fall","become identical","improve permanently"],"key":1,"explanation":"Scores could move up and down on different days.","id":"v17"},{"n":18,"text":"In Passage 2, <u>hypothesis</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["equipment used for measurement","proposed explanation to be tested","rule already enforced","conclusion that cannot change"],"key":1,"explanation":"The team was testing an explanation about predictable sounds.","id":"v18"},{"n":19,"text":"In Passage 2, <u>minor</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["difficult to measure","similar in every way","small in importance or size","caused deliberately"],"key":2,"explanation":"The differences were small, although further monitoring was needed.","id":"v19"},{"n":20,"text":"In Passage 2, <u>monitor</u> is closest in meaning to:","options":["compare only once","bring to an end","track over time","make public immediately"],"key":2,"explanation":"The team kept checking performance for several weeks.","id":"v20"},{"n":21,"text":"The redesigned classroom can <u>accommodate</u> forty students without becoming crowded.<br>Choose the closest meaning of the underlined word.","options":["charge a fee to","hold comfortably","replace permanently","teach independently"],"key":1,"explanation":"The room has enough comfortable space for forty students.","id":"v21"},{"n":22,"text":"The report <u>acknowledges</u> that its conclusions are based on a limited sample.<br>Choose the closest meaning of the underlined word.","options":["conceals","denies","predicts","admits"],"key":3,"explanation":"The report openly accepts this limitation.","id":"v22"},{"n":23,"text":"Some plants can <u>adapt</u> to a drier climate over many generations.<br>Choose the closest meaning of the underlined word.","options":["escape","return","contribute","adjust"],"key":3,"explanation":"They become suited to changed environmental conditions.","id":"v23"},{"n":24,"text":"The evidence is <u>adequate</u> to support a small pilot study, but not a national policy.<br>Choose the closest meaning of the underlined word.","options":["sufficient","confidential","unusual","unnecessary"],"key":0,"explanation":"There is enough evidence for the limited purpose stated.","id":"v24"},{"n":25,"text":"Several teachers <u>advocate</u> giving students feedback before they receive a final grade.<br>Choose the closest meaning of the underlined word.","options":["support","express surprise at","require payment for","postpone"],"key":0,"explanation":"The teachers argue in favor of this approach.","id":"v25"},{"n":26,"text":"Because the instructions were <u>ambiguous</u>, two groups interpreted them differently.<br>Choose the closest meaning of the underlined word.","options":["unusually strict","written in a foreign language","easy to remember","open to more than one meaning"],"key":3,"explanation":"Different reasonable interpretations indicate unclear meaning.","id":"v26"},{"n":27,"text":"The lecturer used an <u>analogy</u> between a city transport network and blood circulation.<br>Choose the closest meaning of the underlined word.","options":["disagreement","experiment","comparison","historical account"],"key":2,"explanation":"Two different systems are compared to explain an idea.","id":"v27"},{"n":28,"text":"The society holds its <u>annual</u> exhibition every November.<br>Choose the closest meaning of the underlined word.","options":["yearly","weekly","permanent","unexpected"],"key":0,"explanation":"An annual event takes place once each year.","id":"v28"},{"n":29,"text":"Researchers <u>derive</u> information about ancient climates from layers of ice.<br>Choose the closest meaning of the underlined word.","options":["hide","protect","obtain","remove completely"],"key":2,"explanation":"They obtain information by studying ice layers.","id":"v29"},{"n":30,"text":"The benefits of extra practice <u>diminish</u> when students are too tired to concentrate.<br>Choose the closest meaning of the underlined word.","options":["appear suddenly","remain equal","spread widely","become smaller"],"key":3,"explanation":"Tired students gain less benefit from additional practice.","id":"v30"},{"n":31,"text":"Volunteers will <u>distribute</u> reusable bottles to everyone at the event.<br>Choose the closest meaning of the underlined word.","options":["describe","give out","manufacture","collect back"],"key":1,"explanation":"The bottles will be handed out to participants.","id":"v31"},{"n":32,"text":"The project brought together a <u>diverse</u> group of students from many backgrounds.<br>Choose the closest meaning of the underlined word.","options":["newly qualified","closely related","varied","highly experienced"],"key":2,"explanation":"Many backgrounds make the group varied.","id":"v32"},{"n":33,"text":"The new filter will <u>eliminate</u> most of the dust from the air entering the room.<br>Choose the closest meaning of the underlined word.","options":["produce","measure","identify","remove"],"key":3,"explanation":"The filter takes most dust out of the incoming air.","id":"v33"},{"n":34,"text":"A small grant will <u>enable</u> the team to buy the equipment it needs.<br>Choose the closest meaning of the underlined word.","options":["make it possible for","make it unnecessary for","remind","force"],"key":0,"explanation":"The grant provides the means to make the purchase.","id":"v34"},{"n":35,"text":"Adding clear examples can <u>enhance</u> the quality of an explanation.<br>Choose the closest meaning of the underlined word.","options":["improve","test","replace","restrict"],"key":0,"explanation":"Examples can make the explanation better.","id":"v35"},{"n":36,"text":"The two courses require an <u>equivalent</u> amount of independent study.<br>Choose the closest meaning of the underlined word.","options":["increasing","equal","limited","unknown"],"key":1,"explanation":"Both courses require the same amount of study.","id":"v36"},{"n":37,"text":"Because the available funding is <u>finite</u>, not every proposal can be supported.<br>Choose the closest meaning of the underlined word.","options":["reliable","shared","flexible","limited"],"key":3,"explanation":"There is a fixed limit to the money available.","id":"v37"},{"n":38,"text":"The policy provides a <u>framework</u> within which individual teachers can plan lessons.<br>Choose the closest meaning of the underlined word.","options":["basic structure","detailed transcript","final examination","financial reward"],"key":0,"explanation":"The policy supplies an organizing structure, not every lesson detail.","id":"v38"},{"n":39,"text":"The revised course will <u>incorporate</u> more practical work alongside lectures.<br>Choose the closest meaning of the underlined word.","options":["replace","include","evaluate","postpone"],"key":1,"explanation":"Practical work becomes part of the course.","id":"v39"},{"n":40,"text":"Some delay was <u>inevitable</u> because every road to the village was flooded.<br>Choose the closest meaning of the underlined word.","options":["unavoidable","unexpected","unimportant","brief"],"key":0,"explanation":"With every road blocked, delay could not be avoided.","id":"v40"},{"n":41,"text":"An <u>objective</u> assessment should depend on evidence rather than the examiner''s personal preferences.<br>Choose the closest meaning of the underlined word.","options":["rapid","favorable","detailed","impartial"],"key":3,"explanation":"An impartial assessment is not biased by personal preference.","id":"v41"},{"n":42,"text":"Lower printing costs helped <u>offset</u> the increase in the price of paper.<br>Choose the closest meaning of the underlined word.","options":["explain","compensate for","predict","add to"],"key":1,"explanation":"Savings in one area counterbalanced an increase in another.","id":"v42"},{"n":43,"text":"Water temperature was the only <u>parameter</u> changed between the two experiments.<br>Choose the closest meaning of the underlined word.","options":["unexpected error","final result","measurable condition","scientific instrument"],"key":2,"explanation":"Temperature was the experimental condition deliberately varied.","id":"v43"},{"n":44,"text":"If the symptoms <u>persist</u> after several days, the patient should seek further advice.<br>Choose the closest meaning of the underlined word.","options":["continue","change location","improve","appear suddenly"],"key":0,"explanation":"Persistent symptoms continue rather than disappear.","id":"v44"},{"n":45,"text":"The interviews offered a different <u>perspective</u> on the neighborhood redevelopment plan.<br>Choose the closest meaning of the underlined word.","options":["set of instructions","point of view","period of time","source of funding"],"key":1,"explanation":"The interviews revealed another way of viewing the plan.","id":"v45"},{"n":46,"text":"Laboratory rules <u>prohibit</u> eating near the research equipment.<br>Choose the closest meaning of the underlined word.","options":["forbid","recommend","require","supervise"],"key":0,"explanation":"The rules do not allow eating in that area.","id":"v46"},{"n":47,"text":"The course in <u>psychology</u> examines how people learn, remember, and make decisions.<br>Choose the closest meaning of the underlined word.","options":["the study of ancient languages","the study of rock formation","the study of mind and behavior","the study of weather patterns"],"key":2,"explanation":"Learning, memory, and decisions are topics within psychology.","id":"v47"},{"n":48,"text":"The coordinator helped the two clubs <u>resolve</u> their disagreement about the room.<br>Choose the closest meaning of the underlined word.","options":["repeat","settle","investigate","report"],"key":1,"explanation":"The clubs reached a solution to their disagreement.","id":"v48"},{"n":49,"text":"The town needs a reliable water supply to <u>sustain</u> its community gardens.<br>Choose the closest meaning of the underlined word.","options":["expand","inspect","advertise","maintain"],"key":3,"explanation":"A dependable water supply keeps the gardens functioning.","id":"v49"},{"n":50,"text":"The survey found <u>widespread</u> support for safer walking routes throughout the city.<br>Choose the closest meaning of the underlined word.","options":["limited to one group","recently withdrawn","existing across many places","expressed very strongly"],"key":2,"explanation":"Support across the city is broadly distributed.","id":"v50"}]},{"id":"reading","title":"Reading","questions":[{"n":1,"text":"Each session will (1) ___ on a different everyday problem.","options":["focus","insist","belong","depend"],"key":0,"explanation":"To focus on something is to give it particular attention.","id":"r1","passage":"The student science center is introducing a series of public workshops. Each session will (1) ___ on a different everyday problem, such as reducing food waste. Participants will work in small groups to (2) ___ possible solutions. No previous experience is (3) ___, so beginners are welcome. The center will provide basic equipment, but visitors should bring a notebook to keep a (4) ___ of their ideas. Since places are limited, everyone must register in (5) ___."},{"n":2,"text":"Participants will work in small groups to (2) ___ possible solutions.","options":["prevent","disappear","borrow","develop"],"key":3,"explanation":"Groups will create or develop solutions to the problems.","id":"r2","passage":"The student science center is introducing a series of public workshops. Each session will (1) ___ on a different everyday problem, such as reducing food waste. Participants will work in small groups to (2) ___ possible solutions. No previous experience is (3) ___, so beginners are welcome. The center will provide basic equipment, but visitors should bring a notebook to keep a (4) ___ of their ideas. Since places are limited, everyone must register in (5) ___."},{"n":3,"text":"No previous experience is (3) ___.","options":["achieved","included","noticed","required"],"key":3,"explanation":"Beginners are welcome, so previous experience is not required.","id":"r3","passage":"The student science center is introducing a series of public workshops. Each session will (1) ___ on a different everyday problem, such as reducing food waste. Participants will work in small groups to (2) ___ possible solutions. No previous experience is (3) ___, so beginners are welcome. The center will provide basic equipment, but visitors should bring a notebook to keep a (4) ___ of their ideas. Since places are limited, everyone must register in (5) ___."},{"n":4,"text":"Visitors should bring a notebook to keep a (4) ___ of their ideas.","options":["record","balance","supply","distance"],"key":0,"explanation":"A notebook can hold a written record of ideas.","id":"r4","passage":"The student science center is introducing a series of public workshops. Each session will (1) ___ on a different everyday problem, such as reducing food waste. Participants will work in small groups to (2) ___ possible solutions. No previous experience is (3) ___, so beginners are welcome. The center will provide basic equipment, but visitors should bring a notebook to keep a (4) ___ of their ideas. Since places are limited, everyone must register in (5) ___."},{"n":5,"text":"Everyone must register in (5) ___.","options":["advance","detail","common","turn"],"key":0,"explanation":"Registering in advance means registering before the event.","id":"r5","passage":"The student science center is introducing a series of public workshops. Each session will (1) ___ on a different everyday problem, such as reducing food waste. Participants will work in small groups to (2) ___ possible solutions. No previous experience is (3) ___, so beginners are welcome. The center will provide basic equipment, but visitors should bring a notebook to keep a (4) ___ of their ideas. Since places are limited, everyone must register in (5) ___."},{"n":6,"text":"What is the main purpose of the email?","options":["To explain changes and preparation for a workshop","To ask students to buy repair tools","To announce that registration has closed","To advertise a series of paid classes"],"key":0,"explanation":"The email announces a new location and explains arrival, items, and cancellation.","id":"r6","passage":"To: Students registered for the Repair and Reuse WorkshopFrom: Campus Activities OfficeSubject: Updated workshop arrangementsThank you for registering for the workshop on Saturday, November 28. Because the courtyard may be too wet, all activities will now take place in Room 104 of the Community Center. The starting time remains 10:00 a.m.Please bring one small, non-electrical household item that needs a simple repair. We will supply tools and spare materials. If you have no suitable item, you may work on one provided by the organizers. Arrive by 9:45 so that a volunteer can check your item before the workshop begins.There is no participation fee. However, students who can no longer attend should cancel through the booking page by Thursday evening. This will allow us to offer their places to people on the waiting list. You do not need to register again to attend at the new location."},{"n":7,"text":"What has changed about the workshop?","options":["Its participation fee","Its location","Its date","Its starting time"],"key":1,"explanation":"Activities moved from the courtyard to Room 104; the 10:00 start is unchanged.","id":"r7","passage":"To: Students registered for the Repair and Reuse WorkshopFrom: Campus Activities OfficeSubject: Updated workshop arrangementsThank you for registering for the workshop on Saturday, November 28. Because the courtyard may be too wet, all activities will now take place in Room 104 of the Community Center. The starting time remains 10:00 a.m.Please bring one small, non-electrical household item that needs a simple repair. We will supply tools and spare materials. If you have no suitable item, you may work on one provided by the organizers. Arrive by 9:45 so that a volunteer can check your item before the workshop begins.There is no participation fee. However, students who can no longer attend should cancel through the booking page by Thursday evening. This will allow us to offer their places to people on the waiting list. You do not need to register again to attend at the new location."},{"n":8,"text":"What should a participant do if they have no suitable item to repair?","options":["Use an item supplied by the organizers","Bring an electrical appliance instead","Cancel their registration","Attend only the final hour"],"key":0,"explanation":"The organizers explicitly offer items to students who have none.","id":"r8","passage":"To: Students registered for the Repair and Reuse WorkshopFrom: Campus Activities OfficeSubject: Updated workshop arrangementsThank you for registering for the workshop on Saturday, November 28. Because the courtyard may be too wet, all activities will now take place in Room 104 of the Community Center. The starting time remains 10:00 a.m.Please bring one small, non-electrical household item that needs a simple repair. We will supply tools and spare materials. If you have no suitable item, you may work on one provided by the organizers. Arrive by 9:45 so that a volunteer can check your item before the workshop begins.There is no participation fee. However, students who can no longer attend should cancel through the booking page by Thursday evening. This will allow us to offer their places to people on the waiting list. You do not need to register again to attend at the new location."},{"n":9,"text":"Why are participants asked to arrive by 9:45?","options":["So they can pay for materials","So they can register a second time","So they can choose the next workshop topic","So a volunteer can check their items"],"key":3,"explanation":"Item checks happen before the workshop begins at 10:00.","id":"r9","passage":"To: Students registered for the Repair and Reuse WorkshopFrom: Campus Activities OfficeSubject: Updated workshop arrangementsThank you for registering for the workshop on Saturday, November 28. Because the courtyard may be too wet, all activities will now take place in Room 104 of the Community Center. The starting time remains 10:00 a.m.Please bring one small, non-electrical household item that needs a simple repair. We will supply tools and spare materials. If you have no suitable item, you may work on one provided by the organizers. Arrive by 9:45 so that a volunteer can check your item before the workshop begins.There is no participation fee. However, students who can no longer attend should cancel through the booking page by Thursday evening. This will allow us to offer their places to people on the waiting list. You do not need to register again to attend at the new location."},{"n":10,"text":"What can be inferred about a student on the waiting list?","options":["They must bring their own tools","They may be offered a place if someone cancels","They should go to the original location","They must pay an additional fee"],"key":1,"explanation":"Prompt cancellation allows organizers to offer the unused place to someone waiting.","id":"r10","passage":"To: Students registered for the Repair and Reuse WorkshopFrom: Campus Activities OfficeSubject: Updated workshop arrangementsThank you for registering for the workshop on Saturday, November 28. Because the courtyard may be too wet, all activities will now take place in Room 104 of the Community Center. The starting time remains 10:00 a.m.Please bring one small, non-electrical household item that needs a simple repair. We will supply tools and spare materials. If you have no suitable item, you may work on one provided by the organizers. Arrive by 9:45 so that a volunteer can check your item before the workshop begins.There is no participation fee. However, students who can no longer attend should cancel through the booking page by Thursday evening. This will allow us to offer their places to people on the waiting list. You do not need to register again to attend at the new location."},{"n":11,"text":"What is the main idea of \"Perception: More Than a Clear Signal\"?","options":["Expectations interfere with perception in every situation","Perception depends on attention and interpretation as well as sensory input","People remember all information that reaches their senses","Louder sounds always prevent perceptual mistakes"],"key":1,"explanation":"All five paragraphs distinguish the physical input from selection and judgment.","id":"r11","passage":"Perception: More Than a Clear Signal1A person searching a crowded station for a friend may look directly at a notice without remembering it. The notice has not necessarily failed to reach the eyes; rather, the person is occupied with another task. Such everyday experiences reveal an important distinction between receiving sensory information and becoming aware of it. Perception is not a complete recording of the environment. It involves selecting information and interpreting what that information might mean.2Attention helps make this selection manageable. At any moment, the senses provide more information than a person can examine closely. Focusing on a particular goal gives some information priority, while other information receives less processing. This limitation can extend across senses: concentrating on a demanding visual task can make an otherwise noticeable sound easier to miss. It would therefore be misleading to interpret every missed sound as evidence that the ears are damaged. The listener''s current activity also matters.3Expectations introduce another influence. Previous experience helps people anticipate what they are likely to encounter. A familiar word may be recognized even when a speaker''s voice is partly covered by noise. In that situation, context makes incomplete information useful. However, the same process can produce errors. Someone expecting a delivery may interpret an unrelated sound as the doorbell. Expectations can improve an interpretation when they fit the evidence, but they can also encourage an interpretation that the evidence does not adequately support.4To investigate such judgments, imagine an experiment in which listeners report whether a faint tone occurred during each trial. Some trials contain a tone, and others contain only background noise. A listener who frequently answers \"yes\" may detect many real tones, but may also report tones that were absent. Another listener may be more cautious and produce fewer false alarms while missing more actual tones. Counting correct detections alone would not provide an adequate comparison. Researchers must also consider the false alarms and the person''s willingness to report an uncertain signal. This example illustrates a decision problem; it does not describe a particular published experiment.5These distinctions have practical implications for warning systems. Simply making a warning louder may help, but does not guarantee that an occupied person will notice or correctly interpret it. Designers need to consider the activity being performed and whether different alerts can be distinguished. An effective warning should attract attention without creating so many unnecessary interruptions that people begin to disregard it. Understanding perception therefore requires both the physical signal and the person receiving it to be considered."},{"n":12,"text":"Why does the author mention the unnoticed station notice in paragraph 1?","options":["To illustrate a difference between receiving information and becoming aware of it","To show that written warnings are less useful than spoken ones","To explain why railway stations need larger signs","To suggest that vision becomes weaker in crowded places"],"key":0,"explanation":"The notice example introduces sensory reception versus conscious awareness.","id":"r12","passage":"Perception: More Than a Clear Signal1A person searching a crowded station for a friend may look directly at a notice without remembering it. The notice has not necessarily failed to reach the eyes; rather, the person is occupied with another task. Such everyday experiences reveal an important distinction between receiving sensory information and becoming aware of it. Perception is not a complete recording of the environment. It involves selecting information and interpreting what that information might mean.2Attention helps make this selection manageable. At any moment, the senses provide more information than a person can examine closely. Focusing on a particular goal gives some information priority, while other information receives less processing. This limitation can extend across senses: concentrating on a demanding visual task can make an otherwise noticeable sound easier to miss. It would therefore be misleading to interpret every missed sound as evidence that the ears are damaged. The listener''s current activity also matters.3Expectations introduce another influence. Previous experience helps people anticipate what they are likely to encounter. A familiar word may be recognized even when a speaker''s voice is partly covered by noise. In that situation, context makes incomplete information useful. However, the same process can produce errors. Someone expecting a delivery may interpret an unrelated sound as the doorbell. Expectations can improve an interpretation when they fit the evidence, but they can also encourage an interpretation that the evidence does not adequately support.4To investigate such judgments, imagine an experiment in which listeners report whether a faint tone occurred during each trial. Some trials contain a tone, and others contain only background noise. A listener who frequently answers \"yes\" may detect many real tones, but may also report tones that were absent. Another listener may be more cautious and produce fewer false alarms while missing more actual tones. Counting correct detections alone would not provide an adequate comparison. Researchers must also consider the false alarms and the person''s willingness to report an uncertain signal. This example illustrates a decision problem; it does not describe a particular published experiment.5These distinctions have practical implications for warning systems. Simply making a warning louder may help, but does not guarantee that an occupied person will notice or correctly interpret it. Designers need to consider the activity being performed and whether different alerts can be distinguished. An effective warning should attract attention without creating so many unnecessary interruptions that people begin to disregard it. Understanding perception therefore requires both the physical signal and the person receiving it to be considered."},{"n":13,"text":"According to paragraph 2, why can a demanding visual task lead to a missed sound?","options":["The task uses attention that might otherwise be available for the sound","The task prevents sound waves from reaching the ears","Visual information always damages auditory memory","People can process information from only one sense throughout their lives"],"key":0,"explanation":"Attention is limited, and a demanding visual task can reduce sound awareness.","id":"r13","passage":"Perception: More Than a Clear Signal1A person searching a crowded station for a friend may look directly at a notice without remembering it. The notice has not necessarily failed to reach the eyes; rather, the person is occupied with another task. Such everyday experiences reveal an important distinction between receiving sensory information and becoming aware of it. Perception is not a complete recording of the environment. It involves selecting information and interpreting what that information might mean.2Attention helps make this selection manageable. At any moment, the senses provide more information than a person can examine closely. Focusing on a particular goal gives some information priority, while other information receives less processing. This limitation can extend across senses: concentrating on a demanding visual task can make an otherwise noticeable sound easier to miss. It would therefore be misleading to interpret every missed sound as evidence that the ears are damaged. The listener''s current activity also matters.3Expectations introduce another influence. Previous experience helps people anticipate what they are likely to encounter. A familiar word may be recognized even when a speaker''s voice is partly covered by noise. In that situation, context makes incomplete information useful. However, the same process can produce errors. Someone expecting a delivery may interpret an unrelated sound as the doorbell. Expectations can improve an interpretation when they fit the evidence, but they can also encourage an interpretation that the evidence does not adequately support.4To investigate such judgments, imagine an experiment in which listeners report whether a faint tone occurred during each trial. Some trials contain a tone, and others contain only background noise. A listener who frequently answers \"yes\" may detect many real tones, but may also report tones that were absent. Another listener may be more cautious and produce fewer false alarms while missing more actual tones. Counting correct detections alone would not provide an adequate comparison. Researchers must also consider the false alarms and the person''s willingness to report an uncertain signal. This example illustrates a decision problem; it does not describe a particular published experiment.5These distinctions have practical implications for warning systems. Simply making a warning louder may help, but does not guarantee that an occupied person will notice or correctly interpret it. Designers need to consider the activity being performed and whether different alerts can be distinguished. An effective warning should attract attention without creating so many unnecessary interruptions that people begin to disregard it. Understanding perception therefore requires both the physical signal and the person receiving it to be considered."},{"n":14,"text":"In paragraph 2, \"priority\" is closest in meaning to:","options":["a longer history","greater importance","complete accuracy","a fixed location"],"key":1,"explanation":"Information given priority receives more attention than competing information.","id":"r14","passage":"Perception: More Than a Clear Signal1A person searching a crowded station for a friend may look directly at a notice without remembering it. The notice has not necessarily failed to reach the eyes; rather, the person is occupied with another task. Such everyday experiences reveal an important distinction between receiving sensory information and becoming aware of it. Perception is not a complete recording of the environment. It involves selecting information and interpreting what that information might mean.2Attention helps make this selection manageable. At any moment, the senses provide more information than a person can examine closely. Focusing on a particular goal gives some information priority, while other information receives less processing. This limitation can extend across senses: concentrating on a demanding visual task can make an otherwise noticeable sound easier to miss. It would therefore be misleading to interpret every missed sound as evidence that the ears are damaged. The listener''s current activity also matters.3Expectations introduce another influence. Previous experience helps people anticipate what they are likely to encounter. A familiar word may be recognized even when a speaker''s voice is partly covered by noise. In that situation, context makes incomplete information useful. However, the same process can produce errors. Someone expecting a delivery may interpret an unrelated sound as the doorbell. Expectations can improve an interpretation when they fit the evidence, but they can also encourage an interpretation that the evidence does not adequately support.4To investigate such judgments, imagine an experiment in which listeners report whether a faint tone occurred during each trial. Some trials contain a tone, and others contain only background noise. A listener who frequently answers \"yes\" may detect many real tones, but may also report tones that were absent. Another listener may be more cautious and produce fewer false alarms while missing more actual tones. Counting correct detections alone would not provide an adequate comparison. Researchers must also consider the false alarms and the person''s willingness to report an uncertain signal. This example illustrates a decision problem; it does not describe a particular published experiment.5These distinctions have practical implications for warning systems. Simply making a warning louder may help, but does not guarantee that an occupied person will notice or correctly interpret it. Designers need to consider the activity being performed and whether different alerts can be distinguished. An effective warning should attract attention without creating so many unnecessary interruptions that people begin to disregard it. Understanding perception therefore requires both the physical signal and the person receiving it to be considered."},{"n":15,"text":"What does the familiar-word example in paragraph 3 demonstrate?","options":["Experience guarantees a correct interpretation","Context can help people interpret incomplete sensory information","A noisy voice cannot communicate familiar words","Hearing becomes independent of physical sound"],"key":1,"explanation":"Context and experience can help a listener recognize a partly obscured word.","id":"r15","passage":"Perception: More Than a Clear Signal1A person searching a crowded station for a friend may look directly at a notice without remembering it. The notice has not necessarily failed to reach the eyes; rather, the person is occupied with another task. Such everyday experiences reveal an important distinction between receiving sensory information and becoming aware of it. Perception is not a complete recording of the environment. It involves selecting information and interpreting what that information might mean.2Attention helps make this selection manageable. At any moment, the senses provide more information than a person can examine closely. Focusing on a particular goal gives some information priority, while other information receives less processing. This limitation can extend across senses: concentrating on a demanding visual task can make an otherwise noticeable sound easier to miss. It would therefore be misleading to interpret every missed sound as evidence that the ears are damaged. The listener''s current activity also matters.3Expectations introduce another influence. Previous experience helps people anticipate what they are likely to encounter. A familiar word may be recognized even when a speaker''s voice is partly covered by noise. In that situation, context makes incomplete information useful. However, the same process can produce errors. Someone expecting a delivery may interpret an unrelated sound as the doorbell. Expectations can improve an interpretation when they fit the evidence, but they can also encourage an interpretation that the evidence does not adequately support.4To investigate such judgments, imagine an experiment in which listeners report whether a faint tone occurred during each trial. Some trials contain a tone, and others contain only background noise. A listener who frequently answers \"yes\" may detect many real tones, but may also report tones that were absent. Another listener may be more cautious and produce fewer false alarms while missing more actual tones. Counting correct detections alone would not provide an adequate comparison. Researchers must also consider the false alarms and the person''s willingness to report an uncertain signal. This example illustrates a decision problem; it does not describe a particular published experiment.5These distinctions have practical implications for warning systems. Simply making a warning louder may help, but does not guarantee that an occupied person will notice or correctly interpret it. Designers need to consider the activity being performed and whether different alerts can be distinguished. An effective warning should attract attention without creating so many unnecessary interruptions that people begin to disregard it. Understanding perception therefore requires both the physical signal and the person receiving it to be considered."},{"n":16,"text":"In paragraph 3, \"the same process\" refers to:","options":["turning up the volume of a speaker","reducing background noise","ignoring all previous experience","using expectations to interpret sensory information"],"key":3,"explanation":"The process just described is interpretation guided by prior experience and expectation.","id":"r16","passage":"Perception: More Than a Clear Signal1A person searching a crowded station for a friend may look directly at a notice without remembering it. The notice has not necessarily failed to reach the eyes; rather, the person is occupied with another task. Such everyday experiences reveal an important distinction between receiving sensory information and becoming aware of it. Perception is not a complete recording of the environment. It involves selecting information and interpreting what that information might mean.2Attention helps make this selection manageable. At any moment, the senses provide more information than a person can examine closely. Focusing on a particular goal gives some information priority, while other information receives less processing. This limitation can extend across senses: concentrating on a demanding visual task can make an otherwise noticeable sound easier to miss. It would therefore be misleading to interpret every missed sound as evidence that the ears are damaged. The listener''s current activity also matters.3Expectations introduce another influence. Previous experience helps people anticipate what they are likely to encounter. A familiar word may be recognized even when a speaker''s voice is partly covered by noise. In that situation, context makes incomplete information useful. However, the same process can produce errors. Someone expecting a delivery may interpret an unrelated sound as the doorbell. Expectations can improve an interpretation when they fit the evidence, but they can also encourage an interpretation that the evidence does not adequately support.4To investigate such judgments, imagine an experiment in which listeners report whether a faint tone occurred during each trial. Some trials contain a tone, and others contain only background noise. A listener who frequently answers \"yes\" may detect many real tones, but may also report tones that were absent. Another listener may be more cautious and produce fewer false alarms while missing more actual tones. Counting correct detections alone would not provide an adequate comparison. Researchers must also consider the false alarms and the person''s willingness to report an uncertain signal. This example illustrates a decision problem; it does not describe a particular published experiment.5These distinctions have practical implications for warning systems. Simply making a warning louder may help, but does not guarantee that an occupied person will notice or correctly interpret it. Designers need to consider the activity being performed and whether different alerts can be distinguished. An effective warning should attract attention without creating so many unnecessary interruptions that people begin to disregard it. Understanding perception therefore requires both the physical signal and the person receiving it to be considered."},{"n":17,"text":"In the imagined experiment, which response is a false alarm?","options":["Reporting a tone that was present","Remaining cautious before giving a response","Reporting a tone during a trial containing only background noise","Failing to report a tone that was present"],"key":2,"explanation":"A false alarm is a reported signal that was actually absent.","id":"r17","passage":"Perception: More Than a Clear Signal1A person searching a crowded station for a friend may look directly at a notice without remembering it. The notice has not necessarily failed to reach the eyes; rather, the person is occupied with another task. Such everyday experiences reveal an important distinction between receiving sensory information and becoming aware of it. Perception is not a complete recording of the environment. It involves selecting information and interpreting what that information might mean.2Attention helps make this selection manageable. At any moment, the senses provide more information than a person can examine closely. Focusing on a particular goal gives some information priority, while other information receives less processing. This limitation can extend across senses: concentrating on a demanding visual task can make an otherwise noticeable sound easier to miss. It would therefore be misleading to interpret every missed sound as evidence that the ears are damaged. The listener''s current activity also matters.3Expectations introduce another influence. Previous experience helps people anticipate what they are likely to encounter. A familiar word may be recognized even when a speaker''s voice is partly covered by noise. In that situation, context makes incomplete information useful. However, the same process can produce errors. Someone expecting a delivery may interpret an unrelated sound as the doorbell. Expectations can improve an interpretation when they fit the evidence, but they can also encourage an interpretation that the evidence does not adequately support.4To investigate such judgments, imagine an experiment in which listeners report whether a faint tone occurred during each trial. Some trials contain a tone, and others contain only background noise. A listener who frequently answers \"yes\" may detect many real tones, but may also report tones that were absent. Another listener may be more cautious and produce fewer false alarms while missing more actual tones. Counting correct detections alone would not provide an adequate comparison. Researchers must also consider the false alarms and the person''s willingness to report an uncertain signal. This example illustrates a decision problem; it does not describe a particular published experiment.5These distinctions have practical implications for warning systems. Simply making a warning louder may help, but does not guarantee that an occupied person will notice or correctly interpret it. Designers need to consider the activity being performed and whether different alerts can be distinguished. An effective warning should attract attention without creating so many unnecessary interruptions that people begin to disregard it. Understanding perception therefore requires both the physical signal and the person receiving it to be considered."},{"n":18,"text":"Why would counting correct detections alone give an inadequate comparison?","options":["Researchers cannot count correct responses accurately","The experiment contains no trials without a tone","A listener may detect more tones simply by reporting them more readily","Every listener hears a different set of background sounds"],"key":2,"explanation":"A ready yes-response can increase hits and false alarms without proving better sensitivity.","id":"r18","passage":"Perception: More 