tual tones. Counting correct detections alone would not provide an adequate comparison. Researchers must also consider the false alarms and the person''s willingness to report an uncertain signal. This example illustrates a decision problem; it does not describe a particular published experiment.5These distinctions have practical implications for warning systems. Simply making a warning louder may help, but does not guarantee that an occupied person will notice or correctly interpret it. Designers need to consider the activity being performed and whether different alerts can be distinguished. An effective warning should attract attention without creating so many unnecessary interruptions that people begin to disregard it. Understanding perception therefore requires both the physical signal and the person receiving it to be considered."},{"n":19,"text":"Which change would the author most likely support in a warning system?","options":["Relying entirely on a user''s expectation of danger","Making every warning equally loud and identical","Making alerts distinguishable while considering the user''s ongoing task","Adding frequent alerts regardless of their importance"],"key":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 supports task-sensitive design and clearly distinguishable alerts.","id":"r19","passage":"Perception: More Than a Clear Signal1A person searching a crowded station for a friend may look directly at a notice without remembering it. The notice has not necessarily failed to reach the eyes; rather, the person is occupied with another task. Such everyday experiences reveal an important distinction between receiving sensory information and becoming aware of it. Perception is not a complete recording of the environment. It involves selecting information and interpreting what that information might mean.2Attention helps make this selection manageable. At any moment, the senses provide more information than a person can examine closely. Focusing on a particular goal gives some information priority, while other information receives less processing. This limitation can extend across senses: concentrating on a demanding visual task can make an otherwise noticeable sound easier to miss. It would therefore be misleading to interpret every missed sound as evidence that the ears are damaged. The listener''s current activity also matters.3Expectations introduce another influence. Previous experience helps people anticipate what they are likely to encounter. A familiar word may be recognized even when a speaker''s voice is partly covered by noise. In that situation, context makes incomplete information useful. However, the same process can produce errors. Someone expecting a delivery may interpret an unrelated sound as the doorbell. Expectations can improve an interpretation when they fit the evidence, but they can also encourage an interpretation that the evidence does not adequately support.4To investigate such judgments, imagine an experiment in which listeners report whether a faint tone occurred during each trial. Some trials contain a tone, and others contain only background noise. A listener who frequently answers \"yes\" may detect many real tones, but may also report tones that were absent. Another listener may be more cautious and produce fewer false alarms while missing more actual tones. Counting correct detections alone would not provide an adequate comparison. Researchers must also consider the false alarms and the person''s willingness to report an uncertain signal. This example illustrates a decision problem; it does not describe a particular published experiment.5These distinctions have practical implications for warning systems. Simply making a warning louder may help, but does not guarantee that an occupied person will notice or correctly interpret it. Designers need to consider the activity being performed and whether different alerts can be distinguished. An effective warning should attract attention without creating so many unnecessary interruptions that people begin to disregard it. Understanding perception therefore requires both the physical signal and the person receiving it to be considered."},{"n":20,"text":"Which statement best describes the organization of the passage?","options":["Two experiments are compared to prove that expectations are always harmful","A design problem is solved by a sequence of mechanical instructions","An everyday example introduces factors in perception, followed by a measurement example and an application","A historical theory is rejected and replaced with a new medical treatment"],"key":2,"explanation":"The passage moves from a station example through attention, expectation, judgment, and warning design.","id":"r20","passage":"Perception: More Than a Clear Signal1A person searching a crowded station for a friend may look directly at a notice without remembering it. The notice has not necessarily failed to reach the eyes; rather, the person is occupied with another task. Such everyday experiences reveal an important distinction between receiving sensory information and becoming aware of it. Perception is not a complete recording of the environment. It involves selecting information and interpreting what that information might mean.2Attention helps make this selection manageable. At any moment, the senses provide more information than a person can examine closely. Focusing on a particular goal gives some information priority, while other information receives less processing. This limitation can extend across senses: concentrating on a demanding visual task can make an otherwise noticeable sound easier to miss. It would therefore be misleading to interpret every missed sound as evidence that the ears are damaged. The listener''s current activity also matters.3Expectations introduce another influence. Previous experience helps people anticipate what they are likely to encounter. A familiar word may be recognized even when a speaker''s voice is partly covered by noise. In that situation, context makes incomplete information useful. However, the same process can produce errors. Someone expecting a delivery may interpret an unrelated sound as the doorbell. Expectations can improve an interpretation when they fit the evidence, but they can also encourage an interpretation that the evidence does not adequately support.4To investigate such judgments, imagine an experiment in which listeners report whether a faint tone occurred during each trial. Some trials contain a tone, and others contain only background noise. A listener who frequently answers \"yes\" may detect many real tones, but may also report tones that were absent. Another listener may be more cautious and produce fewer false alarms while missing more actual tones. Counting correct detections alone would not provide an adequate comparison. Researchers must also consider the false alarms and the person''s willingness to report an uncertain signal. This example illustrates a decision problem; it does not describe a particular published experiment.5These distinctions have practical implications for warning systems. Simply making a warning louder may help, but does not guarantee that an occupied person will notice or correctly interpret it. Designers need to consider the activity being performed and whether different alerts can be distinguished. An effective warning should attract attention without creating so many unnecessary interruptions that people begin to disregard it. Understanding perception therefore requires both the physical signal and the person receiving it to be considered."},{"n":21,"text":"What is the main purpose of \"Reconstructing an Asteroid from Its Fragments\"?","options":["To describe how museums manufacture convincing meteorite displays","To explain how multiple kinds of evidence reveal meteorite histories despite limitations","To argue that laboratory work can replace all observations of space","To show that all meteorites come from the same asteroid"],"key":1,"explanation":"The passage considers differentiation, comparisons, alteration, and collection bias as parts of reconstruction.","id":"r21","passage":"Reconstructing an Asteroid from Its Fragments1A meteorite displayed in a museum looks like an individual object, yet its scientific value often depends on connections to other samples. Many meteorites are fragments of larger bodies that once existed in space. By comparing their materials, scientists can investigate the histories of those parent bodies. The question is not simply what a rock contains, but what processes could have produced that particular combination of minerals and metals.2One important distinction concerns heating. Some small bodies never melted throughout, so their fragments preserve mixtures of early solar-system materials. Others became hot enough for substantial melting and separation to occur. In such a differentiated body, dense metal could sink toward the center, while rocky materials formed outer layers. Later collisions could break the body apart. Consequently, a fragment consisting mainly of iron might sample an ancient interior, whereas a rocky fragment might represent a different layer of the same original body.3Appearance alone, however, cannot reliably establish that two samples share an origin. Similar materials may develop in different places, and related fragments may look very different after experiencing different conditions. Scientists therefore compare several kinds of evidence, including mineral composition and chemical patterns. Matching patterns can strengthen the hypothesis of a shared source. A convincing explanation must also account for differences between the samples rather than selecting only their similarities.4The journey to Earth adds a further complication. Atmospheric entry can melt a thin outer surface, producing a dark fusion crust without melting the entire interior. After landing, exposure to terrestrial water and air may alter the sample again. Researchers therefore distinguish changes that happened in space from changes acquired on Earth. Fresh interior material can provide evidence that a weathered surface no longer preserves. Careful handling and a record of where a specimen was found help researchers evaluate how reliable that evidence is.5Finally, meteorite collections are not necessarily a balanced sample of everything in space. Some fragments survive atmospheric entry and weathering more successfully than others. Certain types are also easier to recognize on the ground. An abundant type in a museum collection therefore need not have been equally abundant in the original population of parent bodies. Combining laboratory evidence with observations of asteroids helps scientists address this limitation. Each sample supplies a partial record; reconstructing the larger history requires several independent clues to be considered together."},{"n":22,"text":"According to paragraph 2, what happens during differentiation?","options":["All minerals become identical without being heated","Collisions always remove every outer layer of a body","Earth''s atmosphere changes a rock into metal","Materials separate into layers after substantial melting"],"key":3,"explanation":"Melting permits dense metal to sink and rocky material to form outer layers.","id":"r22","passage":"Reconstructing an Asteroid from Its Fragments1A meteorite displayed in a museum looks like an individual object, yet its scientific value often depends on connections to other samples. Many meteorites are fragments of larger bodies that once existed in space. By comparing their materials, scientists can investigate the histories of those parent bodies. The question is not simply what a rock contains, but what processes could have produced that particular combination of minerals and metals.2One important distinction concerns heating. Some small bodies never melted throughout, so their fragments preserve mixtures of early solar-system materials. Others became hot enough for substantial melting and separation to occur. In such a differentiated body, dense metal could sink toward the center, while rocky materials formed outer layers. Later collisions could break the body apart. Consequently, a fragment consisting mainly of iron might sample an ancient interior, whereas a rocky fragment might represent a different layer of the same original body.3Appearance alone, however, cannot reliably establish that two samples share an origin. Similar materials may develop in different places, and related fragments may look very different after experiencing different conditions. Scientists therefore compare several kinds of evidence, including mineral composition and chemical patterns. Matching patterns can strengthen the hypothesis of a shared source. A convincing explanation must also account for differences between the samples rather than selecting only their similarities.4The journey to Earth adds a further complication. Atmospheric entry can melt a thin outer surface, producing a dark fusion crust without melting the entire interior. After landing, exposure to terrestrial water and air may alter the sample again. Researchers therefore distinguish changes that happened in space from changes acquired on Earth. Fresh interior material can provide evidence that a weathered surface no longer preserves. Careful handling and a record of where a specimen was found help researchers evaluate how reliable that evidence is.5Finally, meteorite collections are not necessarily a balanced sample of everything in space. Some fragments survive atmospheric entry and weathering more successfully than others. Certain types are also easier to recognize on the ground. An abundant type in a museum collection therefore need not have been equally abundant in the original population of parent bodies. Combining laboratory evidence with observations of asteroids helps scientists address this limitation. Each sample supplies a partial record; reconstructing the larger history requires several independent clues to be considered together."},{"n":23,"text":"What can be inferred about two fragments from different layers of one parent body?","options":["They must be visually identical","They cannot be found on Earth","They must have formed after reaching Earth","They may have different compositions despite sharing an origin"],"key":3,"explanation":"Different layers can yield iron-rich and rocky fragments from the same body.","id":"r23","passage":"Reconstructing an Asteroid from Its Fragments1A meteorite displayed in a museum looks like an individual object, yet its scientific value often depends on connections to other samples. Many meteorites are fragments of larger bodies that once existed in space. By comparing their materials, scientists can investigate the histories of those parent bodies. The question is not simply what a rock contains, but what processes could have produced that particular combination of minerals and metals.2One important distinction concerns heating. Some small bodies never melted throughout, so their fragments preserve mixtures of early solar-system materials. Others became hot enough for substantial melting and separation to occur. In such a differentiated body, dense metal could sink toward the center, while rocky materials formed outer layers. Later collisions could break the body apart. Consequently, a fragment consisting mainly of iron might sample an ancient interior, whereas a rocky fragment might represent a different layer of the same original body.3Appearance alone, however, cannot reliably establish that two samples share an origin. Similar materials may develop in different places, and related fragments may look very different after experiencing different conditions. Scientists therefore compare several kinds of evidence, including mineral composition and chemical patterns. Matching patterns can strengthen the hypothesis of a shared source. A convincing explanation must also account for differences between the samples rather than selecting only their similarities.4The journey to Earth adds a further complication. Atmospheric entry can melt a thin outer surface, producing a dark fusion crust without melting the entire interior. After landing, exposure to terrestrial water and air may alter the sample again. Researchers therefore distinguish changes that happened in space from changes acquired on Earth. Fresh interior material can provide evidence that a weathered surface no longer preserves. Careful handling and a record of where a specimen was found help researchers evaluate how reliable that evidence is.5Finally, meteorite collections are not necessarily a balanced sample of everything in space. Some fragments survive atmospheric entry and weathering more successfully than others. Certain types are also easier to recognize on the ground. An abundant type in a museum collection therefore need not have been equally abundant in the original population of parent bodies. Combining laboratory evidence with observations of asteroids helps scientists address this limitation. Each sample supplies a partial record; reconstructing the larger history requires several independent clues to be considered together."},{"n":24,"text":"In paragraph 3, \"account for\" is closest in meaning to:","options":["leave out","pay for","count repeatedly","explain"],"key":3,"explanation":"A good explanation must explain the differences as well as the similarities.","id":"r24","passage":"Reconstructing an Asteroid from Its Fragments1A meteorite displayed in a museum looks like an individual object, yet its scientific value often depends on connections to other samples. Many meteorites are fragments of larger bodies that once existed in space. By comparing their materials, scientists can investigate the histories of those parent bodies. The question is not simply what a rock contains, but what processes could have produced that particular combination of minerals and metals.2One important distinction concerns heating. Some small bodies never melted throughout, so their fragments preserve mixtures of early solar-system materials. Others became hot enough for substantial melting and separation to occur. In such a differentiated body, dense metal could sink toward the center, while rocky materials formed outer layers. Later collisions could break the body apart. Consequently, a fragment consisting mainly of iron might sample an ancient interior, whereas a rocky fragment might represent a different layer of the same original body.3Appearance alone, however, cannot reliably establish that two samples share an origin. Similar materials may develop in different places, and related fragments may look very different after experiencing different conditions. Scientists therefore compare several kinds of evidence, including mineral composition and chemical patterns. Matching patterns can strengthen the hypothesis of a shared source. A convincing explanation must also account for differences between the samples rather than selecting only their similarities.4The journey to Earth adds a further complication. Atmospheric entry can melt a thin outer surface, producing a dark fusion crust without melting the entire interior. After landing, exposure to terrestrial water and air may alter the sample again. Researchers therefore distinguish changes that happened in space from changes acquired on Earth. Fresh interior material can provide evidence that a weathered surface no longer preserves. Careful handling and a record of where a specimen was found help researchers evaluate how reliable that evidence is.5Finally, meteorite collections are not necessarily a balanced sample of everything in space. Some fragments survive atmospheric entry and weathering more successfully than others. Certain types are also easier to recognize on the ground. An abundant type in a museum collection therefore need not have been equally abundant in the original population of parent bodies. Combining laboratory evidence with observations of asteroids helps scientists address this limitation. Each sample supplies a partial record; reconstructing the larger history requires several independent clues to be considered together."},{"n":25,"text":"Why does the author caution against relying on appearance alone?","options":["All visual differences were created by museum staff","Chemical evidence is unavailable for meteorites","Unrelated samples can look similar, while related samples can look different","Every meteorite has exactly the same surface color"],"key":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 explicitly contrasts outward resemblance with evidence of shared origin.","id":"r25","passage":"Reconstructing an Asteroid from Its Fragments1A meteorite displayed in a museum looks like an individual object, yet its scientific value often depends on connections to other samples. Many meteorites are fragments of larger bodies that once existed in space. By comparing their materials, scientists can investigate the histories of those parent bodies. The question is not simply what a rock contains, but what processes could have produced that particular combination of minerals and metals.2One important distinction concerns heating. Some small bodies never melted throughout, so their fragments preserve mixtures of early solar-system materials. Others became hot enough for substantial melting and separation to occur. In such a differentiated body, dense metal could sink toward the center, while rocky materials formed outer layers. Later collisions could break the body apart. Consequently, a fragment consisting mainly of iron might sample an ancient interior, whereas a rocky fragment might represent a different layer of the same original body.3Appearance alone, however, cannot reliably establish that two samples share an origin. Similar materials may develop in different places, and related fragments may look very different after experiencing different conditions. Scientists therefore compare several kinds of evidence, including mineral composition and chemical patterns. Matching patterns can strengthen the hypothesis of a shared source. A convincing explanation must also account for differences between the samples rather than selecting only their similarities.4The journey to Earth adds a further complication. Atmospheric entry can melt a thin outer surface, producing a dark fusion crust without melting the entire interior. After landing, exposure to terrestrial water and air may alter the sample again. Researchers therefore distinguish changes that happened in space from changes acquired on Earth. Fresh interior material can provide evidence that a weathered surface no longer preserves. Careful handling and a record of where a specimen was found help researchers evaluate how reliable that evidence is.5Finally, meteorite collections are not necessarily a balanced sample of everything in space. Some fragments survive atmospheric entry and weathering more successfully than others. Certain types are also easier to recognize on the ground. An abundant type in a museum collection therefore need not have been equally abundant in the original population of parent bodies. Combining laboratory evidence with observations of asteroids helps scientists address this limitation. Each sample supplies a partial record; reconstructing the larger history requires several independent clues to be considered together."},{"n":26,"text":"According to paragraph 4, a fusion crust forms when:","options":["Atmospheric entry melts a thin outer surface","A metal core separates inside a parent body","A meteorite''s entire interior melts on the ground","Terrestrial plants grow over an old specimen"],"key":0,"explanation":"The passage identifies surface melting during entry as the source of the crust.","id":"r26","passage":"Reconstructing an Asteroid from Its Fragments1A meteorite displayed in a museum looks like an individual object, yet its scientific value often depends on connections to other samples. Many meteorites are fragments of larger bodies that once existed in space. By comparing their materials, scientists can investigate the histories of those parent bodies. The question is not simply what a rock contains, but what processes could have produced that particular combination of minerals and metals.2One important distinction concerns heating. Some small bodies never melted throughout, so their fragments preserve mixtures of early solar-system materials. Others became hot enough for substantial melting and separation to occur. In such a differentiated body, dense metal could sink toward the center, while rocky materials formed outer layers. Later collisions could break the body apart. Consequently, a fragment consisting mainly of iron might sample an ancient interior, whereas a rocky fragment might represent a different layer of the same original body.3Appearance alone, however, cannot reliably establish that two samples share an origin. Similar materials may develop in different places, and related fragments may look very different after experiencing different conditions. Scientists therefore compare several kinds of evidence, including mineral composition and chemical patterns. Matching patterns can strengthen the hypothesis of a shared source. A convincing explanation must also account for differences between the samples rather than selecting only their similarities.4The journey to Earth adds a further complication. Atmospheric entry can melt a thin outer surface, producing a dark fusion crust without melting the entire interior. After landing, exposure to terrestrial water and air may alter the sample again. Researchers therefore distinguish changes that happened in space from changes acquired on Earth. Fresh interior material can provide evidence that a weathered surface no longer preserves. Careful handling and a record of where a specimen was found help researchers evaluate how reliable that evidence is.5Finally, meteorite collections are not necessarily a balanced sample of everything in space. Some fragments survive atmospheric entry and weathering more successfully than others. Certain types are also easier to recognize on the ground. An abundant type in a museum collection therefore need not have been equally abundant in the original population of parent bodies. Combining laboratory evidence with observations of asteroids helps scientists address this limitation. Each sample supplies a partial record; reconstructing the larger history requires several independent clues to be considered together."},{"n":27,"text":"Why can fresh interior material be especially useful to researchers?","options":["It contains no minerals or metals","It was necessarily formed more recently than the surface","It may preserve evidence altered or lost at the surface","It proves that the rock came from a known asteroid"],"key":2,"explanation":"Exposure can alter a surface, while the interior may retain earlier evidence.","id":"r27","passage":"Reconstructing an Asteroid from Its Fragments1A meteorite displayed in a museum looks like an individual object, yet its scientific value often depends on connections to other samples. Many meteorites are fragments of larger bodies that once existed in space. By comparing their materials, scientists can investigate the histories of those parent bodies. The question is not simply what a rock contains, but what processes could have produced that particular combination of minerals and metals.2One important distinction concerns heating. Some small bodies never melted throughout, so their fragments preserve mixtures of early solar-system materials. Others became hot enough for substantial melting and separation to occur. In such a differentiated body, dense metal could sink toward the center, while rocky materials formed outer layers. Later collisions could break the body apart. Consequently, a fragment consisting mainly of iron might sample an ancient interior, whereas a rocky fragment might represent a different layer of the same original body.3Appearance alone, however, cannot reliably establish that two samples share an origin. Similar materials may develop in different places, and related fragments may look very different after experiencing different conditions. Scientists therefore compare several kinds of evidence, including mineral composition and chemical patterns. Matching patterns can strengthen the hypothesis of a shared source. A convincing explanation must also account for differences between the samples rather than selecting only their similarities.4The journey to Earth adds a further complication. Atmospheric entry can melt a thin outer surface, producing a dark fusion crust without melting the entire interior. After landing, exposure to terrestrial water and air may alter the sample again. Researchers therefore distinguish changes that happened in space from changes acquired on Earth. Fresh interior material can provide evidence that a weathered surface no longer preserves. Careful handling and a record of where a specimen was found help researchers evaluate how reliable that evidence is.5Finally, meteorite collections are not necessarily a balanced sample of everything in space. Some fragments survive atmospheric entry and weathering more successfully than others. Certain types are also easier to recognize on the ground. An abundant type in a museum collection therefore need not have been equally abundant in the original population of parent bodies. Combining laboratory evidence with observations of asteroids helps scientists address this limitation. Each sample supplies a partial record; reconstructing the larger history requires several independent clues to be considered together."},{"n":28,"text":"All of the following are mentioned as influences on a collection''s composition EXCEPT:","options":["Survival during atmospheric entry","The price museums pay for different types","How easily fragments are recognized on the ground","Resistance to weathering"],"key":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 names survival, weathering, and recognition, but not museum prices.","id":"r28","passage":"Reconstructing an Asteroid from Its Fragments1A meteorite displayed in a museum looks like an individual object, yet its scientific value often depends on connections to other samples. Many meteorites are fragments of larger bodies that once existed in space. By comparing their materials, scientists can investigate the histories of those parent bodies. The question is not simply what a rock contains, but what processes could have produced that particular combination of minerals and metals.2One important distinction concerns heating. Some small bodies never melted throughout, so their fragments preserve mixtures of early solar-system materials. Others became hot enough for substantial melting and separation to occur. In such a differentiated body, dense metal could sink toward the center, while rocky materials formed outer layers. Later collisions could break the body apart. Consequently, a fragment consisting mainly of iron might sample an ancient interior, whereas a rocky fragment might represent a different layer of the same original body.3Appearance alone, however, cannot reliably establish that two samples share an origin. Similar materials may develop in different places, and related fragments may look very different after experiencing different conditions. Scientists therefore compare several kinds of evidence, including mineral composition and chemical patterns. Matching patterns can strengthen the hypothesis of a shared source. A convincing explanation must also account for differences between the samples rather than selecting only their similarities.4The journey to Earth adds a further complication. Atmospheric entry can melt a thin outer surface, producing a dark fusion crust without melting the entire interior. After landing, exposure to terrestrial water and air may alter the sample again. Researchers therefore distinguish changes that happened in space from changes acquired on Earth. Fresh interior material can provide evidence that a weathered surface no longer preserves. Careful handling and a record of where a specimen was found help researchers evaluate how reliable that evidence is.5Finally, meteorite collections are not necessarily a balanced sample of everything in space. Some fragments survive atmospheric entry and weathering more successfully than others. Certain types are also easier to recognize on the ground. An abundant type in a museum collection therefore need not have been equally abundant in the original population of parent bodies. Combining laboratory evidence with observations of asteroids helps scientists address this limitation. Each sample supplies a partial record; reconstructing the larger history requires several independent clues to be considered together."},{"n":29,"text":"What can be inferred from the discussion of abundant museum types?","options":["Recognizable fragments provide no useful scientific evidence","Rare museum types cannot survive atmospheric entry","Collection frequency alone cannot establish original abundance in space","The most common museum type must have had the largest parent body"],"key":2,"explanation":"Survival and discovery biases mean collection frequency may not mirror original abundance.","id":"r29","passage":"Reconstructing an Asteroid from Its Fragments1A meteorite displayed in a museum looks like an individual object, yet its scientific value often depends on connections to other samples. Many meteorites are fragments of larger bodies that once existed in space. By comparing their materials, scientists can investigate the histories of those parent bodies. The question is not simply what a rock contains, but what processes could have produced that particular combination of minerals and metals.2One important distinction concerns heating. Some small bodies never melted throughout, so their fragments preserve mixtures of early solar-system materials. Others became hot enough for substantial melting and separation to occur. In such a differentiated body, dense metal could sink toward the center, while rocky materials formed outer layers. Later collisions could break the body apart. Consequently, a fragment consisting mainly of iron might sample an ancient interior, whereas a rocky fragment might represent a different layer of the same original body.3Appearance alone, however, cannot reliably establish that two samples share an origin. Similar materials may develop in different places, and related fragments may look very different after experiencing different conditions. Scientists therefore compare several kinds of evidence, including mineral composition and chemical patterns. Matching patterns can strengthen the hypothesis of a shared source. A convincing explanation must also account for differences between the samples rather than selecting only their similarities.4The journey to Earth adds a further complication. Atmospheric entry can melt a thin outer surface, producing a dark fusion crust without melting the entire interior. After landing, exposure to terrestrial water and air may alter the sample again. Researchers therefore distinguish changes that happened in space from changes acquired on Earth. Fresh interior material can provide evidence that a weathered surface no longer preserves. Careful handling and a record of where a specimen was found help researchers evaluate how reliable that evidence is.5Finally, meteorite collections are not necessarily a balanced sample of everything in space. Some fragments survive atmospheric entry and weathering more successfully than others. Certain types are also easier to recognize on the ground. An abundant type in a museum collection therefore need not have been equally abundant in the original population of parent bodies. Combining laboratory evidence with observations of asteroids helps scientists address this limitation. Each sample supplies a partial record; reconstructing the larger history requires several independent clues to be considered together."},{"n":30,"text":"How does the final paragraph contribute to the passage?","options":["It gives instructions for finding every missing parent body","It identifies a sampling limitation and explains why laboratory and asteroid evidence should be combined","It argues that only iron-rich samples should be preserved","It proves that earlier chemical comparisons were unnecessary"],"key":1,"explanation":"The conclusion addresses biased collections and advocates combining independent clues.","id":"r30","passage":"Reconstructing an Asteroid from Its Fragments1A meteorite displayed in a museum looks like an individual object, yet its scientific value often depends on connections to other samples. Many meteorites are fragments of larger bodies that once existed in space. By comparing their materials, scientists can investigate the histories of those parent bodies. The question is not simply what a rock contains, but what processes could have produced that particular combination of minerals and metals.2One important distinction concerns heating. Some small bodies never melted throughout, so their fragments preserve mixtures of early solar-system materials. Others became hot enough for substantial melting and separation to occur. In such a differentiated body, dense metal could sink toward the center, while rocky materials formed outer layers. Later collisions could break the body apart. Consequently, a fragment consisting mainly of iron might sample an ancient interior, whereas a rocky fragment might represent a different layer of the same original body.3Appearance alone, however, cannot reliably establish that two samples share an origin. Similar materials may develop in different places, and related fragments may look very different after experiencing different conditions. Scientists therefore compare several kinds of evidence, including mineral composition and chemical patterns. Matching patterns can strengthen the hypothesis of a shared source. A convincing explanation must also account for differences between the samples rather than selecting only their similarities.4The journey to Earth adds a further complication. Atmospheric entry can melt a thin outer surface, producing a dark fusion crust without melting the entire interior. After landing, exposure to terrestrial water and air may alter the sample again. Researchers therefore distinguish changes that happened in space from changes acquired on Earth. Fresh interior material can provide evidence that a weathered surface no longer preserves. Careful handling and a record of where a specimen was found help researchers evaluate how reliable that evidence is.5Finally, meteorite collections are not necessarily a balanced sample of everything in space. Some fragments survive atmospheric entry and weathering more successfully than others. Certain types are also easier to recognize on the ground. An abundant type in a museum collection therefore need not have been equally abundant in the original population of parent bodies. Combining laboratory evidence with observations of asteroids helps scientists address this limitation. Each sample supplies a partial record; reconstructing the larger history requires several independent clues to be considered together."}]}],"arrangements":[{"context":"What will the new grant allow the team to do?","answer":"The grant will enable us to monitor local air quality.","tokens":["to monitor","The grant will enable","local air quality","us"],"acceptedOrders":[[1,3,0,2]]},{"context":"Why did the students move their meeting?","answer":"They needed a room that could accommodate everyone.","tokens":["everyone","a room","They needed","that could accommodate"],"acceptedOrders":[[2,1,3,0]]},{"context":"When should we decide how to spend the budget?","answer":"We should evaluate the proposals before we allocate the funds.","tokens":["the proposals","the funds","before we allocate","We should evaluate"],"acceptedOrders":[[3,0,2,1]]},{"context":"Why did the researcher repeat the experiment?","answer":"She wanted to establish whether the results were reliable.","tokens":["whether","the results were reliable","to establish","She wanted"],"acceptedOrders":[[3,2,0,1]]},{"context":"How can the teacher help us understand the concept?","answer":"An accurate diagram can enhance our understanding of the concept.","tokens":["can enhance","our understanding","An accurate diagram","of the concept"],"acceptedOrders":[[2,0,1,3]]},{"context":"What did the coordinator ask about the repair workshop?","answer":"He asked whether we had adequate equipment for the workshop.","tokens":["adequate equipment","He asked","for the workshop","whether we had"],"acceptedOrders":[[1,3,0,2],[2,1,3,0]]},{"context":"Why should both groups discuss the plan?","answer":"A different perspective may help us resolve the disagreement.","tokens":["resolve the disagreement","may help","A different perspective","us"],"acceptedOrders":[[2,1,3,0]]},{"context":"What is the main aim of the new course?","answer":"It aims to incorporate practical experience into academic study.","tokens":["to incorporate","into academic study","practical experience","It aims"],"acceptedOrders":[[3,0,2,1]]},{"context":"What did the report say about classroom noise?","answer":"It acknowledged that external noise could affect concentration.","tokens":["could affect","concentration","that external noise","It acknowledged"],"acceptedOrders":[[3,2,0,1]]},{"context":"Why is long-term funding important?","answer":"Reliable funding is necessary to sustain the community project.","tokens":["is necessary","to sustain","Reliable funding","the community project"],"acceptedOrders":[[2,0,1,3],[1,3,2,0]]}],"tasks":[{"id":"writing-email","section":"writing","title":"Email","answerId":"email","prompt":"You have 7 minutes. Write in complete sentences and address all three points.Your student club is organizing a Repair and Reuse Workshop on November 28. The campus equipment office agreed to provide ten tool kits, but the delivery contains only four. Twenty students are registered, and the missing equipment may prevent some of them from participating.Write an email to Ms. Rivera, the equipment coordinator. In your email:Describe the difference between the agreed order and the delivery.Explain how the shortage could affect the workshop.Request a practical solution and suggest an alternative if the remaining kits are unavailable.To: Ms. RiveraSubject: Equipment for the Repair and Reuse WorkshopYour Email0 words","rubric":"writing"},{"id":"writing-discussion","section":"writing","title":"Academic Discussion","answerId":"discussion","prompt":"You have 10 minutes. Write at least 100 words. Support your position with reasons and an example.Professor Chen:Universities have a finite budget for helping students improve their writing. Some want to invest more in AI systems that provide immediate feedback. Others prefer to fund additional small-group sessions with teachers. Which investment should receive priority, and why?Amira:I would prioritize AI feedback. It can highlight problems whenever a student is studying, not only during office hours. Teachers could then use class time to address more complex questions.Leo:I would fund more teacher-led sessions. A teacher can ask what a student intended to say and help the student evaluate an argument. Automated comments may be accurate about grammar but less useful about the quality of ideas.Contribute your own view to the discussion. Develop a clear position with reasons and an example. You may acknowledge another perspective, but do not simply repeat the students'' comments. Use linking words to connect your ideas.Your Discussion Post0 words","rubric":"writing"},{"id":"repeat-1","section":"speaking","title":"Repeat Sentence 1","prompt":"Welcome to the Community Repair Center.","target":"Welcome to the Community Repair Center.","rubric":"repeat"},{"id":"repeat-2","section":"speaking","title":"Repeat Sentence 2","prompt":"The workshop is in the adjacent building.","target":"The workshop is in the adjacent building.","rubric":"repeat"},{"id":"repeat-3","section":"speaking","title":"Repeat Sentence 3","prompt":"We provide adequate equipment for every group.","target":"We provide adequate equipment for every group.","rubric":"repeat"},{"id":"repeat-4","section":"speaking","title":"Repeat Sentence 4","prompt":"Please monitor your belongings while you work in the center.","target":"Please monitor your belongings while you work in the center.","rubric":"repeat"},{"id":"repeat-5","section":"speaking","title":"Repeat Sentence 5","prompt":"Our volunteers can adapt each activity to accommodate different levels of experience.","target":"Our volunteers can adapt each activity to accommodate different levels of experience.","rubric":"repeat"},{"id":"repeat-6","section":"speaking","title":"Repeat Sentence 6","prompt":"Before using a tool, make sure that you have received accurate instructions from a member of staff.","target":"Before using a tool, make sure that you have received accurate instructions from a member of staff.","rubric":"repeat"},{"id":"repeat-7","section":"speaking","title":"Repeat Sentence 7","prompt":"Although our funding is limited, we aim to sustain this service and incorporate more activities next year.","target":"Although our funding is limited, we aim to sustain this service and incorporate more activities next year.","rubric":"repeat"},{"id":"interview-1","section":"speaking","title":"Interview Question 1","prompt":"We are interviewing people about shared spaces in their communities. Describe a public place that you use, such as a park, library, or community center. What do you usually do there?","rubric":"speaking"},{"id":"interview-2","section":"speaking","title":"Interview Question 2","prompt":"Some people prefer quiet public spaces, while others prefer places with organized activities. Which type do you prefer, and how does that environment affect your experience?","rubric":"speaking"},{"id":"interview-3","section":"speaking","title":"Interview Question 3","prompt":"A town has a finite budget and must choose between improving an existing park and establishing a new community learning center. Which option should it prioritize? Explain your perspective.","rubric":"speaking"},{"id":"interview-4","section":"speaking","title":"Interview Question 4","prompt":"Some community centers use digital tools to monitor attendance and provide information, while others rely mainly on staff. How could centers incorporate technology without diminishing the value of human assistance? Give reasons and an example.","rubric":"speaking"}]}'::jsonb) on conflict(exam_id) do update set version=excluded.version,content=excluded.content;
